            {"type": "score", "title": "Q11 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Analyser in polarization", "body": "Know first: Effects of polariser and analyser; Malus's law. An analyser is a second polarizing device (such as a Polaroid or Nicol prism) placed in the path of a light beam to determine whether the beam is polarized and to find its plane of polarization. When rotated about the beam axis, the transmitted intensity of linearly polarized light varies as \\( I = I_0 \\cos^2\\theta \\), giving complete extinction twice per revolution.", "tex": "I = I_0 \\cos^2\\theta"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Action of an analyser", "tex": "E_{\\text{out}} = E_0 \\cos\\theta \\\\ I = I_0 \\cos^2\\theta", "body": "Project the incident polarized amplitude onto the pass-axis of the analyser at angle theta. Definition 1 · Malus's law 1."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "A polarizing device used to test the state of polarization of a light beam is called an ____.", "options": ["analyser", "achromat", "interferometer", "collimator"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "The first device is the polarizer and the second is the analyser."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "When linearly polarized light is viewed through a rotating analyser, complete darkness occurs at ____.", "options": ["two positions per rotation", "one position per rotation", "four positions per rotation", "no position"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "cos^2(theta) = 0 at theta = 90 degrees and 270 degrees."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "The variation of transmitted intensity through an analyser is governed by ____.", "options": ["Malus's law", "Brewster's law", "Snell's law", "Fermat's principle"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Malus's law gives I = I0 cos^2(theta)."}
