          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 5 marks", "title": "Question 16", "marks": 5, "q": "Explain maxima and minima in an N slit Fraunhofer diffraction"},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Intensity formula, principal maxima, minima and secondary maxima", "options": [{"t": "Intensity expression, principal maxima, N-1 minima and N-2 secondary maxima explained", "m": 5}, {"t": "Principal maxima and minima conditions stated without secondary maxima", "m": 3}, {"t": "Only grating equation stated", "m": 1.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Give the N-slit intensity distribution and conditions for principal maxima, minima, and secondary maxima."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q16 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Principal maxima, minima and secondary maxima", "body": "Know first: N-slit Fraunhofer diffraction pattern and grating. The intensity of an \\( N \\)-slit grating is \\( I = I_0 \\frac{\\sin^2\\beta}{\\beta^2}\\frac{\\sin^2 N\\gamma}{\\sin^2\\gamma} \\), where \\( \\beta = \\frac{\\pi b\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda} \\) and \\( \\gamma = \\frac{\\pi d\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda} \\). Principal maxima of intensity \\( N^2 I_0 \\frac{\\sin^2\\beta}{\\beta^2} \\) occur when \\( \\gamma = m\\pi \\), i.e. \\( d\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda \\). Between two adjacent principal maxima there are \\( N - 1 \\) minima where \\( N\\gamma = p\\pi \\) (\\( p \\neq mN \\)) and \\( N - 2 \\) weak secondary maxima.", "tex": "d\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda \\text{ (principal maxima)}, \\quad N d\\sin\\theta = p\\lambda \\text{ (minima)}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Conditions for N-slit extrema", "tex": "I = I_0 \\left(\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{\\sin N\\gamma}{\\sin\\gamma}\\right)^2, \\quad \\beta = \\frac{\\pi b\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda}, \\quad \\gamma = \\frac{\\pi d\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda} \\\\ \\text{Principal maxima: } \\sin\\gamma = 0 \\implies \\gamma = m\\pi \\implies d\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda, \\quad m = 0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\dots \\\\ \\text{Minima: } \\sin N\\gamma = 0 \\text{ with } \\sin\\gamma \\neq 0 \\implies N\\gamma = p\\pi \\implies d\\sin\\theta = \\frac{p}{N}\\lambda \\\\ p \\neq 0, \\pm N, \\pm 2N, \\dots \\implies (N-1)\\text{ minima and }(N-2)\\text{ secondary maxima}", "body": "State the N-slit intensity equation and solve for zeros and indeterminate forms of the interference factor. Intensity formula 1.5 · principal maxima 1.5 · minima & secondary maxima 2."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Between two adjacent principal maxima in an \\( N \\)-slit pattern, the number of minima is ____.", "options": ["N minus 1", "N minus 2", "N", "2N"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "p takes N-1 integer values between mN and (m+1)N."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Between two consecutive principal maxima of an \\( N \\)-slit grating, the number of secondary maxima is ____.", "options": ["N minus 2", "N minus 1", "N", "N plus 1"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "There is one secondary maximum between each pair of the N-1 minima."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "The peak intensity of the interference factor \\( (\\sin N\\gamma / \\sin\\gamma)^2 \\) at a principal maximum is ____.", "options": ["N squared", "N", "2N", "1 over N"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "By L'Hopital's rule, lim (sin N gamma / sin gamma)^2 = N^2."}
          ]
