          "id": "opt-pyq-n23-q18",
          "title": "Q18 · Huygens' explanation of double refraction (5 marks)",
          "icon": "wave",
          "year": 2023,
          "month": "Nov",
          "unit": 20,
          "units": [20],
          "marks": 5,
          "source": "PYQ Nov 2023 Q18",
          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 5 marks", "title": "Question 18", "marks": 5, "q": "Define and explain Huygen's explanation of double refraction"},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Definition of double refraction and Huygens' spherical/ellipsoidal wavelets", "options": [{"t": "Splitting into o-ray and e-ray defined, and spherical + ellipsoidal wave surfaces explained with optic axis", "m": 5}, {"t": "Double refraction defined and wavelets mentioned without positive/negative crystal detail", "m": 3}, {"t": "Only definition of double refraction given", "m": 1.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Define double refraction and explain Huygens' secondary spherical (o-wave) and ellipsoidal (e-wave) wave surfaces."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q18 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Double refraction and Huygens' wave theory", "body": "Know first: Double refraction and Huygens' explanation. Double refraction (birefringence) is the splitting of an unpolarized light beam entering an anisotropic crystal (like calcite or quartz) into two linearly polarized beams: the ordinary ray (o-ray), which obeys Snell's law, and the extraordinary ray (e-ray), which generally does not. According to Huygens, each point in a uniaxial crystal emits two secondary wave surfaces: a sphere of speed \\( v_o = c/n_o \\) for the o-ray and an ellipsoid of revolution for the e-ray touching the sphere along the optic axis.", "tex": "v_{ro} = \\frac{c}{n_o}, \\quad \\frac{1}{v_{re}^2} = \\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{(c/n_e)^2} + \\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{(c/n_o)^2}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Huygens' ray velocity surfaces", "tex": "v_{ro} = \\frac{c}{n_o} \\quad (\\text{sphere for o-ray}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{v_{re}^2} = \\frac{\\cos^2\\theta}{(c/n_o)^2} + \\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{(c/n_e)^2} \\quad (\\text{ellipsoid of revolution for e-ray}) \\\\ \\theta = 0 \\text{ (along optic axis)}: \\; v_{re} = v_{ro} = \\frac{c}{n_o} \\\\ \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}: \\; v_{re} = \\frac{c}{n_e} \\quad (n_e < n_o \\Rightarrow v_{re} > v_{ro} \\text{ negative; } n_e > n_o \\Rightarrow v_{re} < v_{ro} \\text{ positive})", "body": "Define o-ray and e-ray, write their velocity equations, and compare the spherical and ellipsoidal wave surfaces. Definition 1.5 · Huygens postulates 2 · positive/negative crystals 1.5."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In Huygens' theory of double refraction, the secondary wave surface for the ordinary ray is a ____.", "options": ["sphere", "ellipsoid of revolution", "paraboloid", "cylinder"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "The ordinary ray travels with the same velocity c/n_o in all directions."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Along the optic axis of a uniaxial crystal, the velocities of the o-ray and e-ray are ____.", "options": ["equal", "perpendicular", "zero", "infinite"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "The sphere and ellipsoid touch each other along the optic axis."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In a negative uniaxial crystal like calcite, the ellipsoid of revolution lies ____.", "options": ["outside the sphere", "inside the sphere", "perpendicular to the sphere", "at infinity"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "For a negative crystal n_e < n_o, so v_re >= v_ro and the ellipsoid encloses the sphere."}
          ]
        },
