            {"type": "score", "title": "Q20 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Gaussian refraction formula and spherical mirror equation", "body": "Know first: Refraction and reflection at a single spherical surface. For paraxial refraction at a spherical surface of radius \\( R \\) separating media \\( n_1 \\) and \\( n_2 \\), the Gaussian formula is \\( \\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R} \\). For reflection at a spherical mirror \\( SPM \\), using \\( \\phi_1 = \\phi_2 \\) and small-angle approximations \\( \\phi_1 = \\beta - \\alpha_1 \\approx \\frac{h}{r} - \\frac{h}{x} \\) and \\( \\phi_2 = \\alpha_2 - \\beta \\approx \\frac{h}{y} - \\frac{h}{r} \\) with \\( u = -x, v = -y, R = -r \\), we obtain \\( \\frac{1}{u} + \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{2}{R} \\).", "tex": "\\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}, \\quad \\frac{1}{u} + \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{2}{R}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Gaussian refraction and spherical reflection derivation", "tex": "\\text{Refraction (Gaussian formula): } \\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R} \\\\ \\text{Reflection at spherical mirror } SPM: \\quad \\phi_1 = \\beta - \\alpha_1 \\approx \\frac{h}{r} - \\frac{h}{x} \\\\ \\phi_2 = \\alpha_2 - \\beta \\approx \\frac{h}{y} - \\frac{h}{r} \\\\ \\text{Law of reflection } \\phi_1 = \\phi_2 \\implies \\frac{h}{r} - \\frac{h}{x} = \\frac{h}{y} - \\frac{h}{r} \\implies \\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{y} = \\frac{2}{r} \\\\ \\text{Sign convention } (u = -x,\\; v = -y,\\; R = -r): \\quad \\frac{1}{u} + \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{2}{R}", "body": "State and explain the Gaussian formula for refraction, then use exterior-angle geometry and the law of reflection to derive the mirror equation. Gaussian formula 5 · figure 2 · reflection derivation 3."},
            {
              "type": "derivation",
              "label": "Derivation",
              "title": "Reflection by a single spherical surface",
              "legend": "Paraxial ray OS from object O reflects at S on spherical mirror SPM (center C, pole P) to image I.",
              "setup": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 260 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"15\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><path d=\"M 195 25 Q 230 115 195 165\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1a73e8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"35\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"206\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><line x1=\"105\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"206\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,3\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><line x1=\"206\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"145\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><line x1=\"206\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"206\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-dasharray=\"2,2\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"130\" font-size=\"10\">O</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"130\" font-size=\"10\">C</text><text x=\"142\" y=\"130\" font-size=\"10\">I</text><text x=\"202\" y=\"130\" font-size=\"10\">D</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"130\" font-size=\"10\">P</text><text x=\"208\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"10\">S</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"10\">h</text><text x=\"58\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"9\">α1</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"9\">β</text><text x=\"153\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"9\">α2</text></svg>",
              "phases": ["Exterior angles", "Paraxial approximation", "Law of reflection", "Sign convention"],
              "steps": [
                {"phase": 0, "math": "\\phi_1 = \\beta - \\alpha_1, \\quad \\phi_2 = \\alpha_2 - \\beta", "note": "Relate angles of incidence and reflection to axial angles in triangles OSC and CSI."},
                {"phase": 1, "math": "\\phi_1 \\approx \\frac{h}{r} - \\frac{h}{x}, \\quad \\phi_2 \\approx \\frac{h}{y} - \\frac{h}{r}", "note": "Apply small-angle tangent approximations with foot D close to pole P."},
                {"phase": 2, "math": "\\frac{h}{r} - \\frac{h}{x} = \\frac{h}{y} - \\frac{h}{r} \\implies \\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{y} = \\frac{2}{r}", "note": "Equate phi1 and phi2 by the law of reflection and cancel h."},
                {"phase": 3, "math": "u = -x, \\; v = -y, \\; R = -r \\implies \\frac{1}{u} + \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{2}{R}", "note": "Substitute Cartesian signs for distances to the left of pole P."}
              ],
              "ask": [
                {"q": "In triangle OSC, how is the angle of incidence \\( \\phi_1 \\) related to \\( \\beta \\) and \\( \\alpha_1 \\)?", "options": ["beta minus alpha1", "beta plus alpha1", "alpha1 minus beta", "2 beta"], "answer": 0, "why": "Exterior angle beta equals alpha1 + phi1."},
                {"q": "Under the paraxial approximation, what replaces \\( \\alpha_1, \\beta, \\alpha_2 \\)?", "options": ["h/x, h/r, h/y", "x/h, r/h, y/h", "h*x, h*r, h*y", "r/x, r/y, x/y"], "answer": 0, "why": "For small angles, angle in radians equals tangent h / distance."},
                {"q": "Which physical law equates \\( \\phi_1 \\) and \\( \\phi_2 \\) to give \\( 1/x + 1/y = 2/r \\)?", "options": ["Law of reflection", "Brewster's law", "Malus's law", "Rayleigh's criterion"], "answer": 0, "why": "Angle of incidence equals angle of reflection."},
                {"q": "Since O, C and I lie to the left of pole P, what substitution gives the mirror equation?", "options": ["u = -x, v = -y, R = -r", "u = +x, v = -y, R = +r", "u = -x, v = +y, R = +r", "u = x, v = y, R = 2r"], "answer": 0, "why": "All distances to the left of P are negative."}
