          "id": "opt-pyq-n22o-q5",
          "title": "Q5 · Intensity distribution in N-slit Fraunhofer diffraction (2 marks)",
          "icon": "wave",
          "year": 2022,
          "month": "Nov",
          "unit": 14,
          "units": [14],
          "marks": 2,
          "source": "PYQ Nov 2022 (CBCSS) Q5",
          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 2 marks", "title": "Question 5", "marks": 2, "q": "Give an expression for the intensity distribution in Fraunhofer diffraction due to N slits and explain the terms involved."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "N-slit intensity equation and definition of beta and gamma", "options": [{"t": "Formula I = I0 (sin beta / beta)^2 (sin N gamma / sin gamma)^2 and terms explained", "m": 2}, {"t": "Formula only without explaining beta and gamma", "m": 1}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Write the product of diffraction and interference factors and define the symbols."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q5 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "N-slit Fraunhofer intensity formula", "body": "Know first: N-slit Fraunhofer diffraction pattern and grating. The intensity at diffraction angle \\( \\theta \\) for \\( N \\) parallel slits of width \\( b \\) and grating element \\( d \\) is \\( I = I_0 \\left(\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{\\sin N\\gamma}{\\sin\\gamma}\\right)^2 \\). Here \\( \\beta = \\frac{\\pi b\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda} \\) is the single-slit diffraction parameter and \\( \\gamma = \\frac{\\pi d\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda} \\) is half the phase difference between adjacent slits.", "tex": "I = I_0 \\left(\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{\\sin N\\gamma}{\\sin\\gamma}\\right)^2"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Definitions of diffraction and interference factors", "tex": "I = I_0 \\frac{\\sin^2\\beta}{\\beta^2}\\frac{\\sin^2 N\\gamma}{\\sin^2\\gamma} \\\\ \\beta = \\frac{\\pi b\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda}, \\quad \\gamma = \\frac{\\pi d\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda}", "body": "State the complete intensity expression and define b (slit width), d (grating spacing), N (slit count) and lambda (wavelength). Formula 1 · terms 1."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In the \\( N \\)-slit intensity formula, the factor \\( (\\sin\\beta / \\beta)^2 \\) represents ____.", "options": ["single-slit diffraction", "multi-slit interference", "polarization loss", "chromatic dispersion"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "(sin beta / beta)^2 is the envelope due to diffraction from each individual slit."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In the \\( N \\)-slit pattern, \\( \\gamma = \\pi d\\sin\\theta / \\lambda \\) depends on the ____.", "options": ["slit separation d", "slit height", "lens thickness", "screen width"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "2 gamma is the phase difference between rays from corresponding points of adjacent slits."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "When \\( N = 1 \\), the \\( N \\)-slit intensity formula reduces to the ____.", "options": ["single-slit diffraction formula", "Young double-slit formula", "Brewster law", "Malus law"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "For N = 1, (sin N gamma / sin gamma)^2 = 1."}
          ]
        },
