          "id": "opt-pyq-n22o-q13",
          "title": "Q13 · Thin lens and thin lens formula (5 marks)",
          "icon": "wave",
          "year": 2022,
          "month": "Nov",
          "unit": 3,
          "units": [3],
          "marks": 5,
          "source": "PYQ Nov 2022 (CBCSS) Q13",
          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 5 marks", "title": "Question 13", "marks": 5, "q": "What do you mean by a thin lens? Write down the thin lens formula and explain the terms involved."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Definition of thin lens, lens maker's / Gaussian formula, and symbol definitions", "options": [{"t": "Thin lens defined, 1/v - 1/u = (n-1)(1/R1 - 1/R2) = 1/f written, and all terms explained", "m": 5}, {"t": "Formula and terms given without defining thin lens", "m": 3}, {"t": "Only formula written", "m": 1.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Define a thin spherical lens, write the thin lens equation, and explain u, v, f, n, R1, R2."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q13 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Thin lens definition and formula", "body": "Know first: The thin lens, principal foci and focal length. A thin lens is a transparent medium bounded by two spherical refracting surfaces whose axial thickness \\( t \\) is negligible compared to the object distance \\( u \\), image distance \\( v \\), and radii of curvature \\( R_1, R_2 \\). Its paraxial imaging obeys the thin lens formula \\( \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = (n - 1)\\left(\\frac{1}{R_1} - \\frac{1}{R_2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{f} \\), where \\( n = n_2/n_1 \\) is the relative refractive index and \\( f \\) is the focal length.", "tex": "\\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = (n - 1)\\left(\\frac{1}{R_1} - \\frac{1}{R_2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{f}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Thin lens equations and terms", "tex": "\\frac{n_2}{v'} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R_1}, \\quad \\frac{n_1}{v} - \\frac{n_2}{v'} = \\frac{n_1 - n_2}{R_2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = (n - 1)\\left(\\frac{1}{R_1} - \\frac{1}{R_2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{f}, \\quad n = \\frac{n_2}{n_1}", "body": "Define a thin lens, write both forms of the thin lens formula, and identify u, v, f, n = n2/n1, R1, and R2. Definition 1.5 · formula 2 · terms 1.5."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In the thin lens formula, \\( R_1 \\) and \\( R_2 \\) are the ____.", "options": ["radii of curvature of the two surfaces", "distances of the two foci", "heights of object and image", "refractive indices of the media"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "R1 and R2 denote the radii of curvature of the left and right spherical surfaces."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "For a biconvex glass lens in air, \\( R_1 \\) is positive and \\( R_2 \\) is ____.", "options": ["negative", "positive", "zero", "infinite"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "The center of curvature C2 of the right surface lies to the left of the lens."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "If a glass biconvex lens is immersed in a liquid of higher refractive index than glass, it acts as a ____.", "options": ["diverging lens", "converging lens", "plane mirror", "polarizer"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "When n = n2/n1 < 1, (n - 1) becomes negative, reversing the sign of f."}
          ]
        },
