          "unit": 22,
          "units": [22],
          "marks": 10,
          "source": "PYQ Nov 2022 (CBCSS) Q21",
          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 10 marks", "title": "Question 21", "marks": 10, "q": "Using suitable figures, explain the optical activity of substances. Discuss the Fresnel's explanation of optical rotation."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Explanation of optical activity, dextro/laevo substances and figure", "options": [{"t": "Optical activity defined, dextro/laevo distinguished, and vector rotation diagrams drawn", "m": 4}, {"t": "Definition given without diagrams", "m": 2}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Explain optical activity and draw the rotating electric field / circular components."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Fresnel's theory of RCP and LCP superposition and rotation angle derivation", "options": [{"t": "Decomposition into RCP and LCP waves with speeds c/nR, c/nL and derivation of phi = (pi/lambda)(nL - nR)z", "m": 6}, {"t": "Qualitative statement of Fresnel's assumptions without full derivation", "m": 3}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Derive the rotation angle phi = (pi/lambda)(nL - nR)z by superposing RCP and LCP waves."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q21 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Optical activity and Fresnel's theory of rotation", "body": "Know first: Production and analysis of polarised light (22). Optical activity is the rotation of the plane of vibration of linearly polarized light propagating through chiral media (like quartz along its optic axis or sugar solution). According to Fresnel, linearly polarized light is a superposition of equal-amplitude right-circularly polarized (RCP) and left-circularly polarized (LCP) waves that propagate with slightly different refractive indices \\( n_R \\) and \\( n_L \\). After distance \\( z \\), their phase difference \\( (k_L - k_R)z \\) rotates the resultant linear polarization by \\( \\phi = \\frac{\\pi}{\\lambda}(n_L - n_R)z \\).", "tex": "\\phi(z) = \\frac{1}{2}(k_L - k_R)z = \\frac{\\pi}{\\lambda}(n_L - n_R)z"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Fresnel's mathematical derivation of optical rotation", "tex": "E_x^R = E_0\\cos(k_R z - \\omega t), \\quad E_y^R = -E_0\\sin(k_R z - \\omega t) \\quad (\\text{RCP},\\; k_R = \\frac{\\omega}{c}n_R) \\\\ E_x^L = E_0\\cos(k_L z - \\omega t), \\quad E_y^L = +E_0\\sin(k_L z - \\omega t) \\quad (\\text{LCP},\\; k_L = \\frac{\\omega}{c}n_L) \\\\ E_x = E_x^R + E_x^L = 2E_0\\cos\\left[\\frac{1}{2}(k_L - k_R)z\\right]\\cos\\left[\\omega t - \\theta(z)\\right] \\\\ E_y = E_y^R + E_y^L = 2E_0\\sin\\left[\\frac{1}{2}(k_L - k_R)z\\right]\\cos\\left[\\omega t - \\theta(z)\\right] \\\\ \\tan\\phi = \\frac{E_y}{E_x} = \\tan\\left[\\frac{1}{2}(k_L - k_R)z\\right] \\implies \\phi(z) = \\frac{\\pi}{\\lambda}(n_L - n_R)z", "body": "Express the incident x-polarized wave as the sum of RCP and LCP waves with wave numbers kR and kL, add components at depth z, and obtain the rotation angle phi. Optical activity & diagrams 4 · Fresnel derivation 6."},
            {
              "type": "derivation",
              "label": "Derivation",
              "title": "Fresnel's theory of optical rotation",
              "legend": "Superposition of counter-rotating RCP (phase phi1 = kR z) and LCP (phase phi2 = kL z) vectors yielding rotated LP wave at angle phi.",
              "setup": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 260 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><circle cx=\"55\" cy=\"95\" r=\"32\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1a73e8\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><line x1=\"55\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"55\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"82\" y2=\"78\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><circle cx=\"135\" cy=\"95\" r=\"32\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#188038\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><line x1=\"135\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"135\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"135\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"67\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"192\" y1=\"125\" x2=\"228\" y2=\"65\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"42\" y=\"42\" font-size=\"10\">RCP (kR)</text><text x=\"92\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"12\">+</text><text x=\"122\" y=\"42\" font-size=\"10\">LCP (kL)</text><text x=\"175\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"12\">=</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"42\" font-size=\"10\">Rotated LP</text><text x=\"213\" y=\"75\" font-size=\"9\">ϕ</text></svg>",
              "phases": ["Circular decomposition", "Independent propagation", "Field superposition", "Rotation angle phi"],
              "steps": [
                {"phase": 0, "math": "E_x(0,t) = 2E_0\\cos\\omega t = E_0\\cos\\omega t + E_0\\cos\\omega t, \\quad E_y(0,t) = -E_0\\sin\\omega t + E_0\\sin\\omega t = 0", "note": "Decompose the incident linearly polarized wave at z = 0 into RCP and LCP waves of equal amplitude E0."},
                {"phase": 1, "math": "k_R = \\frac{\\omega}{c}n_R, \\quad k_L = \\frac{\\omega}{c}n_L", "note": "Let the RCP and LCP normal modes propagate through distance z with different refractive indices nR and nL."},
                {"phase": 2, "math": "E_x = 2E_0\\cos\\left[\\frac{1}{2}(k_L - k_R)z\\right]\\cos(\\omega t - \\theta), \\quad E_y = 2E_0\\sin\\left[\\frac{1}{2}(k_L - k_R)z\\right]\\cos(\\omega t - \\theta)", "note": "Sum the x and y components at depth z using trigonometric sum-to-product identities."},
                {"phase": 3, "math": "\\phi(z) = \\frac{1}{2}(k_L - k_R)z = \\frac{\\pi}{\\lambda}(n_L - n_R)z", "note": "Identify the angle phi that the resultant linear oscillation makes with the x-axis."}
              ],
              "ask": [
                {"q": "Into what two waves does Fresnel's theory resolve an incident linearly polarized beam?", "options": ["Equal-amplitude RCP and LCP waves", "Two unpolarized waves", "Longitudinal and transverse waves", "Diffracted and reflected waves"], "answer": 0, "why": "The normal modes of an optically active medium are RCP and LCP waves."},
                {"q": "Why do the RCP and LCP components acquire a phase difference after distance \\( z \\)?", "options": ["They travel with slightly different speeds (nL != nR)", "One component is absorbed completely", "They travel in perpendicular directions", "Their frequencies change"], "answer": 0, "why": "Circular birefringence means nL != nR, so kL != kR."},
                {"q": "What is the resultant polarization state when the RCP and LCP waves recombine at depth \\( z \\)?", "options": ["Linearly polarized, rotated by angle phi", "Unpolarized", "Circularly polarized", "Zero intensity"], "answer": 0, "why": "Since both components still have equal amplitude E0, their sum is linearly polarized at angle phi."},
                {"q": "What is the condition on \\( n_L \\) and \\( n_R \\) for a substance to be dextrorotatory (right-handed)?", "options": ["nL greater than nR", "nL less than nR", "nL equal to nR", "nL + nR = 0"], "answer": 0, "why": "When nL > nR, the RCP wave travels faster and the plane rotates clockwise (phi > 0)."}
              ]
            },
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "According to Fresnel, optical rotation is due to circular ____.", "options": ["birefringence (nL != nR)", "dichroism", "diffraction", "reflection"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Different refractive indices for LCP and RCP waves rotate the plane of polarization."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In an isotropic optically inactive medium where \\( n_L = n_R \\), the rotation angle \\( \\phi \\) is ____.", "options": ["zero", "90 degrees", "180 degrees", "infinite"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "When nL = nR, phi = (pi/lambda)(nL - nR)z = 0."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "A substance that rotates the plane of polarization anticlockwise (\\( n_L < n_R \\)) is called ____.", "options": ["laevorotatory", "dextrorotatory", "achromatic", "isotropic"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Left-handed (anticlockwise) optical rotation is called laevorotatory."}
          ]
        }
      ]
