            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Optical direction cosine", "body": "Know first: Refraction and reflection at a single spherical surface. The optical direction cosine of a ray at any point in a medium of refractive index \\( n \\) is the product of the refractive index and the cosine of the angle that the ray makes with the coordinate axis (in paraxial matrix optics, \\( \\lambda = n\\cos\\alpha \\approx n\\theta \\), where \\( \\theta \\) is the slope angle with the optical axis). Together with height \\( y \\), it specifies the ray coordinates \\( (y, n\\theta) \\).", "tex": "L = n\\cos\\alpha \\approx n\\theta"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Optical direction cosine in ray coordinates", "tex": "\\lambda = n\\cos\\alpha = n\\sin\\theta \\approx n\\theta \\\\ \\text{Ray vector} = \\begin{bmatrix} y \\\\ n\\theta \\end{bmatrix}", "body": "Define optical direction cosine as n times the direction cosine (or paraxial slope angle theta) used in ray-transfer matrices. Definition 1 · formula/role 1."},
