            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Construction and advantages of a hologram", "body": "Know first: Interference of light waves. To construct a hologram, a broad monochromatic laser beam is split by a beam splitter into two parts: a reference beam directed by a mirror onto a high-resolution photographic plate, and an object beam that illuminates the object so scattered waves reach the same plate. The plate records their stationary interference pattern \\( I = |E_R + E_O|^2 \\). Advantages: (1) records both amplitude and phase to give a true 3D image with parallax, (2) any small fragment reconstructs the whole scene, and (3) multiple images can be stored on one plate.", "tex": "I = I_R + I_O + 2\\sqrt{I_R I_O}\\cos(\\phi_O - \\phi_R)"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Hologram recording equation and advantages", "tex": "E_{\\text{total}} = E_R e^{i\\phi_R} + E_O e^{i\\phi_O} \\\\ I = |E_{\\text{total}}|^2 = E_R^2 + E_O^2 + 2E_R E_O\\cos(\\phi_O - \\phi_R)", "body": "Describe the laser beam-splitting and interference recording setup, then list the advantages (3D parallax, phase recording, fault tolerance, high storage density). Construction 2.5 · advantages 1.5."},
