          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 10 marks", "title": "Question 35", "marks": 10, "q": "Briefly explain an optical fiber. Using ray theory, discuss the mechanism of transmission of light with an optical fiber. Deduce on expression for numerical aperture of an optical fiber."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Structure of optical fiber and total internal reflection ray mechanism", "options": [{"t": "Core-cladding structure and ray guidance via total internal reflection explained with diagram", "m": 5}, {"t": "Brief explanation without ray diagram", "m": 2.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Describe the cylindrical core (n1) and cladding (n2 < n1) and explain total internal reflection."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Derivation of numerical aperture NA = sqrt(n1^2 - n2^2) / n0", "options": [{"t": "Complete derivation using Snell's law at launch face and critical angle at core-cladding boundary", "m": 5}, {"t": "Final formula stated without complete derivation", "m": 2.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Apply Snell's law at the input face and use sin(theta_c) = n2/n1 to deduce NA."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q35 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Optical fiber ray transmission and numerical aperture", "body": "Know first: Laws of reflection and refraction from Fermat's principle. An optical fiber is a flexible dielectric waveguide consisting of a central cylindrical core of refractive index \\( n_1 \\) surrounded by a cladding of lower refractive index \\( n_2 < n_1 \\). Light launched into the core within the acceptance cone strikes the core-cladding interface at an angle greater than the critical angle \\( \\theta_c = \\sin^{-1}(n_2/n_1) \\) and propagates by repeated total internal reflection. Applying Snell's law at the entrance face gives the numerical aperture \\( \\text{NA} = n_0\\sin\\theta_a = \\sqrt{n_1^2 - n_2^2} \\).", "tex": "\\text{NA} = n_0\\sin\\theta_a = \\sqrt{n_1^2 - n_2^2}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Derivation of numerical aperture", "tex": "\\text{At entrance face (medium } n_0 \\text{ to core } n_1\\text{): } n_0\\sin\\theta_a = n_1\\sin\\theta_r \\\\ \\text{At core-cladding interface for limiting ray: } \\theta_r = 90^\\circ - \\theta_c, \\quad \\sin\\theta_c = \\frac{n_2}{n_1} \\\\ \\sin\\theta_r = \\sin(90^\\circ - \\theta_c) = \\cos\\theta_c = \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2\\theta_c} = \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{n_2^2}{n_1^2}} \\\\ \\text{NA} = n_0\\sin\\theta_a = n_1\\sqrt{1 - \\frac{n_2^2}{n_1^2}} = \\sqrt{n_1^2 - n_2^2} \\approx n_1\\sqrt{2\\Delta}", "body": "Explain the core-cladding waveguide and total internal reflection, then combine Snell's law at the input end with the critical-angle condition at the core wall. Fiber & TIR mechanism 5 · NA derivation 5."},
            {
              "type": "derivation",
              "label": "Derivation",
              "title": "Numerical aperture of a step-index optical fiber",
              "legend": "Limiting ray entering fiber core (n1) from medium (n0) at acceptance angle theta_a and meeting cladding (n2) at critical angle theta_c.",
              "setup": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 260 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"70\" y=\"35\" width=\"170\" height=\"25\" fill=\"#e8f0fe\" stroke=\"#555\"/><rect x=\"70\" y=\"60\" width=\"170\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#1a73e8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"70\" y=\"120\" width=\"170\" height=\"25\" fill=\"#e8f0fe\" stroke=\"#555\"/><line x1=\"15\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"245\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"135\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"135\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"135\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"135\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-dasharray=\"2,2\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"105\" font-size=\"9\">θa</text><text x=\"92\" y=\"88\" font-size=\"9\">θr</text><text x=\"122\" y=\"78\" font-size=\"9\">θc</text><text x=\"205\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"10\">Cladding n2</text><text x=\"205\" y=\"108\" font-size=\"10\">Core n1</text></svg>",
              "phases": ["Entrance refraction", "Critical angle condition", "Trigonometric identity", "Numerical aperture"],
              "steps": [
                {"phase": 0, "math": "n_0\\sin\\theta_a = n_1\\sin\\theta_r", "note": "Apply Snell's law to the limiting ray entering the core from outside medium n0."},
                {"phase": 1, "math": "\\sin\\theta_c = \\frac{n_2}{n_1}, \\quad \\theta_r = 90^\\circ - \\theta_c", "note": "Relate the internal refraction angle theta_r to the critical angle theta_c in the right triangle."},
                {"phase": 2, "math": "\\sin\\theta_r = \\cos\\theta_c = \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^2\\theta_c} = \\frac{\\sqrt{n_1^2 - n_2^2}}{n_1}", "note": "Express sin(theta_r) = cos(theta_c) in terms of n1 and n2."},
                {"phase": 3, "math": "\\text{NA} = n_0\\sin\\theta_a = \\sqrt{n_1^2 - n_2^2}", "note": "Substitute sin(theta_r) into Snell's law to obtain the numerical aperture."}
              ],
              "ask": [
                {"q": "What law relates the launch angle \\( \\theta_a \\) in medium \\( n_0 \\) to the internal angle \\( \\theta_r \\) in the core?", "options": ["Snell's law: n0 sin(theta_a) = n1 sin(theta_r)", "Malus's law", "Brewster's law", "Bragg's law"], "answer": 0, "why": "Refraction at the flat input end face obeys Snell's law."},
                {"q": "In the right triangle inside the core, how are \\( \\theta_r \\) and the critical angle \\( \\theta_c \\) related?", "options": ["theta_r + theta_c = 90 degrees", "theta_r = theta_c", "theta_r - theta_c = 90 degrees", "theta_r + theta_c = 180 degrees"], "answer": 0, "why": "The core axis and the normal to the cylindrical wall are perpendicular."},
                {"q": "Since \\( \\sin\\theta_c = n_2 / n_1 \\), what is \\( \\cos\\theta_c \\)?", "options": ["sqrt(n1^2 - n2^2) / n1", "n2 / n1", "n1 / n2", "n1 - n2"], "answer": 0, "why": "cos(theta_c) = sqrt(1 - (n2/n1)^2)."},
                {"q": "What is the resulting expression for \\( \\text{NA} = n_0\\sin\\theta_a \\)?", "options": ["sqrt(n1^2 - n2^2)", "n1^2 - n2^2", "n1 + n2", "n2 / n1"], "answer": 0, "why": "Multiplying n1 by cos(theta_c) cancels n1 in the denominator."}
              ]
            },
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In terms of the fractional refractive index change \\( \\Delta = (n_1^2 - n_2^2)/(2n_1^2) \\), the numerical aperture is ____.", "options": ["n1 sqrt(2 Delta)", "n1 Delta", "2 n1 Delta", "Delta / n1"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "From 2 n1^2 Delta = n1^2 - n2^2 = NA^2, we get NA = n1 sqrt(2 Delta)."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "The outer dielectric layer surrounding the core of an optical fiber is called the ____.", "options": ["cladding", "polarizer", "analyser", "retarder"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "The lower-index cladding confines light to the core by total internal reflection."},
