          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 3 marks", "title": "Question 8", "marks": 3, "q": "How is an elliptically polarized light produced ?"},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Passing linearly polarized light through a quarter-wave plate at angle theta != 0, 45, 90 degrees", "options": [{"t": "Linearly polarized light incident on a QWP at an angle other than 0, 45 or 90 degrees to the optic axis explained", "m": 3}, {"t": "Mentions QWP without specifying the unequal-amplitude angle condition", "m": 1.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Explain how two orthogonal components of unequal amplitude with a pi/2 phase difference form elliptically polarized light."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q8 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Production of elliptically polarized light", "body": "Know first: Production and analysis of polarised light (22). Elliptically polarized light is produced by passing a linearly polarized light beam normally through a quarter-wave plate (QWP) such that the electric vector of the incident beam makes an angle \\( \\phi \\) (other than \\( 0^\\circ, 45^\\circ, 90^\\circ \\)) with the optic axis. Inside the QWP, the beam splits into ordinary and extraordinary components of unequal amplitudes \\( E_0\\sin\\phi \\) and \\( E_0\\cos\\phi \\) with a phase difference of \\( \\pi/2 \\), which recombine on emergence to trace an ellipse.", "tex": "\\frac{E_x^2}{E_0^2\\sin^2\\phi} + \\frac{E_y^2}{E_0^2\\cos^2\\phi} = 1 \\quad (\\phi \\neq 0, \\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{2})"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Ellipse equation from a quarter-wave plate", "tex": "E_x = E_0\\sin\\phi\\sin\\omega t, \\quad E_y = E_0\\cos\\phi\\cos\\omega t \\\\ \\frac{E_x^2}{(E_0\\sin\\phi)^2} + \\frac{E_y^2}{(E_0\\cos\\phi)^2} = \\sin^2\\omega t + \\cos^2\\omega t = 1", "body": "Resolve the incident amplitude E0 along and perpendicular to the optic axis of a QWP with phi != 45 degrees and combine with a pi/2 phase shift. Setup/angle condition 1.5 · wave equation 1.5."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "To produce elliptically polarized light using a quarter-wave plate, the incident linear polarization must make an angle with the optic axis ____.", "options": ["other than 0, 45, or 90 degrees", "equal to 45 degrees", "equal to 0 degrees", "equal to 90 degrees"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "At 45 degrees the amplitudes are equal (giving circular polarization); at 0 or 90 degrees only one wave exists (linear)."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "A quarter-wave plate introduces a phase difference between the o-ray and e-ray equal to ____.", "options": ["pi over 2", "pi", "2 pi", "zero"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "A path difference of lambda/4 corresponds to a phase difference of pi/2."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In elliptically polarized light, the magnitude of the electric field vector ____.", "options": ["varies periodically as it rotates", "remains constant as it rotates", "oscillates along a fixed straight line", "is always zero"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "The tip of the electric vector traces an ellipse, varying between the semi-major and semi-minor axes."}
          ]
        },
