          "id": "opt-pyq-n21o-q9",
          "title": "Q9 · Dextrorotatory vs laevorotatory substances (3 marks)",
          "icon": "wave",
          "year": 2021,
          "month": "Nov",
          "unit": 22,
          "units": [22],
          "marks": 3,
          "source": "PYQ Nov 2021 (CBCSS) Q9",
          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 3 marks", "title": "Question 9", "marks": 3, "q": "Distinguish between dextrorotatory and laevorotatory substances."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Rotation direction, refractive index inequality (nL vs nR), and examples", "options": [{"t": "Clockwise vs anticlockwise rotation (facing the source), nL > nR vs nL < nR, and examples given", "m": 3}, {"t": "Only clockwise vs anticlockwise stated", "m": 1.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Compare rotation sense, circular refractive indices, and examples."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q9 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Dextrorotatory and laevorotatory substances", "body": "Know first: Production and analysis of polarised light (22). Dextrorotatory (right-handed) substances rotate the plane of polarization clockwise when looking towards the oncoming light beam; for them \\( n_L > n_R \\) (RCP travels faster than LCP, e.g., cane sugar solution, d-quartz). Laevorotatory (left-handed) substances rotate the plane of polarization anticlockwise; for them \\( n_L < n_R \\) (LCP travels faster than RCP, e.g., fruit sugar/fructose, l-quartz).", "tex": "\\text{Dextro: } n_L > n_R \\;(\\phi > 0); \\quad \\text{Laevo: } n_L < n_R \\;(\\phi < 0)"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Fresnel rotation sign condition", "tex": "\\phi(z) = \\frac{\\pi}{\\lambda}(n_L - n_R)z \\\\ n_L > n_R \\implies v_R > v_L \\implies \\phi > 0 \\text{ (dextrorotatory)} \\\\ n_L < n_R \\implies v_L > v_R \\implies \\phi < 0 \\text{ (laevorotatory)}", "body": "Contrast the direction of rotation, circular wave velocities, and examples for both classes. Direction of rotation 1.5 · nL vs nR & examples 1.5."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "A substance that rotates the plane of polarization clockwise (looking against the beam) is ____.", "options": ["dextrorotatory", "laevorotatory", "isotropic", "dichroic"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Dextro means right-handed (clockwise)."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In a dextrorotatory medium, the circularly polarized component that travels faster is the ____.", "options": ["right-circularly polarized (RCP) wave", "left-circularly polarized (LCP) wave", "unpolarized wave", "longitudinal wave"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Since nL > nR, vR = c/nR > c/nL = vL."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Quartz crystals exist in both dextrorotatory and laevorotatory forms because of their ____.", "options": ["mirror-image helical crystal structures", "magnetic permeability", "cubic symmetry", "free electrons"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Enantiomorphic quartz crystals have opposite spiral arrangements of SiO2 tetrahedra."}
          ]
        },
