          "title": "Q11 · Step-index vs graded-index optical fibers (3 marks)",
          "icon": "wave",
          "year": 2021,
          "month": "Nov",
          "unit": 1,
          "units": [1],
          "marks": 3,
          "source": "PYQ Nov 2021 (CBCSS) Q11 · also Nov 2020 Q24",
          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 3 marks", "title": "Question 11", "marks": 3, "q": "Distinguish between step index and graded index optical fibers."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Comparison of refractive index profile, ray trajectories, and pulse dispersion", "options": [{"t": "Index profile (uniform vs parabolic), ray path (zig-zag vs sinusoidal), and dispersion/bandwidth compared", "m": 3}, {"t": "Only index difference stated", "m": 1.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Contrast core refractive index profile, ray path geometry, and intermodal dispersion."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q11 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Comparison of step-index and graded-index fibers", "body": "Know first: Laws of reflection and refraction from Fermat's principle. (1) In a step-index fiber, the core refractive index \\( n_1 \\) is uniform throughout and drops abruptly to \\( n_2 \\) at the cladding boundary; in a graded-index (GRIN) fiber, the core index decreases continuously (parabolically) from the axis to \\( n_2 \\). (2) Rays travel in straight zig-zag paths by sharp total internal reflection in step-index fibers, but follow smooth sinusoidal curved paths by continuous refraction in GRIN fibers. (3) GRIN fibers have much lower intermodal pulse dispersion and higher bandwidth.", "tex": "\\text{Step: } n(r) = n_1; \\quad \\text{GRIN: } n^2(r) = n_1^2[1 - 2\\Delta(r/a)^2]"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Dispersion comparison", "tex": "\\Delta\\tau_{\\text{step}} \\approx \\frac{n_1 L}{c}\\Delta, \\quad \\Delta\\tau_{\\text{GRIN}} \\approx \\frac{n_1 L}{2c}\\Delta^2 \\ll \\Delta\\tau_{\\text{step}}", "body": "Compare refractive index distribution, ray trajectories, and modal dispersion. Index profile 1 · ray paths 1 · dispersion/bandwidth 1."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In a step-index optical fiber, the meridional rays propagate along ____.", "options": ["zig-zag straight-line paths", "helical circles", "sinusoidal curves", "parabolic arcs"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Constant core index n1 gives straight ray segments between reflections at the core wall."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In a graded-index fiber, rays travelling farther from the axis move faster because the refractive index there is ____.", "options": ["lower", "higher", "infinite", "negative"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Local phase velocity v = c/n(r) increases as n(r) decreases radially."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "The intermodal pulse broadening in a parabolic graded-index fiber is proportional to ____.", "options": ["Delta squared", "Delta", "1 over Delta", "square root of Delta"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Parabolic grading cancels first-order delay, leaving a small Delta^2 term."}
          ]
        },
