            {"type": "score", "title": "Q12 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Basic components of a fiber optic sensor", "body": "Know first: Laws of reflection and refraction from Fermat's principle. A fiber optic sensor system consists of: (1) an optical source (LED or laser diode) that launches light into the fiber, (2) the optical fiber and sensing/transducer region where the measurand (temperature, pressure, strain, or magnetic field) modulates the intensity, phase, polarization, or wavelength of the guided light, and (3) a photodetector and electronic signal processor that converts the modulated optical signal into a measurable electrical output.", "tex": "\\text{Source} \\to \\text{Fiber / Sensing Modulator} \\to \\text{Detector & Processor}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Signal modulation in fiber sensor", "tex": "E_{\\text{out}} = A(X)\\cos\\left[\\omega(X)t + \\phi(X)\\right], \\quad X = T, P, \\epsilon", "body": "Describe the optical source, the intrinsic/extrinsic fiber sensing element, and the optical detector/demodulator. Source 1 · sensing fiber 1 · detector 1."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In an intrinsic fiber optic sensor, the modulation of the light beam takes place ____.", "options": ["inside the optical fiber itself", "outside the fiber in air", "inside the power supply", "at the oscilloscope screen"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "In an intrinsic sensor, the fiber itself acts as the sensing element."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "The component at the receiving end of a fiber optic sensor that converts light into electrical current is a ____.", "options": ["photodetector", "beam splitter", "Nicol prism", "zone plate"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "A photodiode or photodetector converts optical power into photocurrent."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Fiber optic sensors are ideal for hazardous environments because they are immune to ____.", "options": ["electromagnetic interference", "total internal reflection", "wave propagation", "gravity"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Dielectric glass fibers do not conduct electricity or pick up RF interference."}
