          "id": "opt-pyq-n21o-q13",
          "title": "Q13 · Derivation of Newtonian lens formula (5 marks)",
          "icon": "wave",
          "year": 2021,
          "month": "Nov",
          "unit": 4,
          "units": [4],
          "marks": 5,
          "source": "PYQ Nov 2021 (CBCSS) Q13",
          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 5 marks", "title": "Question 13", "marks": 5, "q": "Obtain the Newtonian lens formula."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Ray diagram and similar-triangle derivation of f1 f2 = x1 x2 (or x1 x2 = -f^2)", "options": [{"t": "Clear ray diagram, similar triangle ratios -y'/y = -f1/(-x1) = x2/f2, and f1 f2 = x1 x2 (x1 x2 = -f^2) derived", "m": 5}, {"t": "Derivation without diagram or missing sign explanation", "m": 3}, {"t": "Only final formula stated", "m": 1.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Use similar triangles at the first and second principal foci to derive f1 f2 = x1 x2."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q13 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Newtonian lens formula f1 f2 = x1 x2", "body": "Know first: The Newton formula and lateral magnification. Let \\( x_1 \\) be the axial distance of the object from the first principal focus \\( F_1 \\) and \\( x_2 \\) be the axial distance of the image from the second principal focus \\( F_2 \\). From similar triangles at \\( F_1 \\) and \\( F_2 \\), \\( \\frac{-y'}{y} = \\frac{-f_1}{-x_1} \\) and \\( \\frac{-y'}{y} = \\frac{x_2}{f_2} \\), which gives \\( f_1 f_2 = x_1 x_2 \\). When the medium on both sides is the same (\\( f_2 = -f_1 = f \\)), this becomes \\( x_1 x_2 = -f^2 \\).", "tex": "f_1 f_2 = x_1 x_2, \\quad x_1 x_2 = -f^2"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Derivation from similar triangles at F1 and F2", "tex": "\\text{From similar triangles } AOF_1 \\text{ and } LPF_1: \\quad \\frac{-y'}{y} = \\frac{-f_1}{-x_1} = \\frac{f_1}{x_1} \\\\ \\text{From similar triangles } KPF_2 \\text{ and } BIF_2: \\quad \\frac{-y'}{y} = \\frac{x_2}{f_2} \\\\ \\frac{f_1}{x_1} = \\frac{x_2}{f_2} \\implies f_1 f_2 = x_1 x_2 \\\\ \\text{For identical media on both sides } (f_2 = -f_1 = f): \\quad x_1 x_2 = -f^2", "body": "Equate the height ratios -y'/y from the two pairs of similar focal triangles. Diagram 1.5 · triangle ratios 2 · final Newton formula 1.5."},
            {
              "type": "derivation",
              "label": "Derivation",
              "title": "Newton's formula for a thin lens",
              "legend": "Object OA at x1 from F1 and image IB at x2 from F2 for a thin lens P.",
              "setup": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 260 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"15\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"245\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#1a73e8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#188038\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"220\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"125\" x2=\"220\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"220\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"220\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#188038\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"103\" font-size=\"9\">O</text><text x=\"35\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"9\">A</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"103\" font-size=\"9\">F1</text><text x=\"125\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"9\">P</text><text x=\"170\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"9\">F2</text><text x=\"218\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"9\">I</text><text x=\"218\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"9\">B</text><text x=\"55\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"9\">-x1</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"9\">-f1</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"103\" font-size=\"9\">f2</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"103\" font-size=\"9\">x2</text></svg>",
              "phases": ["First focal triangles", "Second focal triangles", "Equating ratios", "Identical surrounding media"],
              "steps": [
                {"phase": 0, "math": "\\frac{-y'}{y} = \\frac{-f_1}{-x_1}", "note": "Use similar right triangles AOF1 and LPF1 on the object side."},
                {"phase": 1, "math": "\\frac{-y'}{y} = \\frac{x_2}{f_2}", "note": "Use similar right triangles KPF2 and BIF2 on the image side."},
                {"phase": 2, "math": "\\frac{f_1}{x_1} = \\frac{x_2}{f_2} \\implies f_1 f_2 = x_1 x_2", "note": "Equate the two expressions for -y'/y and cross-multiply."},
                {"phase": 3, "math": "f_2 = -f_1 = f \\implies x_1 x_2 = -f^2", "note": "Substitute f1 = -f and f2 = +f when the lens has the same medium on both sides."}
              ],
              "ask": [
                {"q": "From What reference points are \\( x_1 \\) and \\( x_2 \\) measured in Newton's lens formula?", "options": ["First focus F1 and second focus F2", "Pole P and center of curvature C", "Object O and image I", "Screen and slit"], "answer": 0, "why": "Newton's formula measures extrafocal distances x1 from F1 and x2 from F2."},
                {"q": "Which ratio is obtained from similar triangles \\( KPF_2 \\) and \\( BIF_2 \\)?", "options": ["-y'/y = x2/f2", "-y'/y = f2/x2", "y'/y = x1/f1", "y' = f2"], "answer": 0, "why": "KP = y, IB = -y', PF2 = f2, and F2I = x2."},
                {"q": "What general relation follows by equating the two expressions for \\( -y'/y \\)?", "options": ["f1 f2 = x1 x2", "f1 + f2 = x1 + x2", "f1 / f2 = x1 / x2", "x1 - x2 = f"], "answer": 0, "why": "f1/x1 = x2/f2 cross-multiplies to f1 f2 = x1 x2."},
                {"q": "Why does \\( x_1 x_2 = -f^2 \\) imply that \\( x_1 \\) and \\( x_2 \\) always have opposite signs?", "options": ["Their product is negative (-f^2 < 0)", "Their sum is zero", "f is imaginary", "x1 is always zero"], "answer": 0, "why": "Since -f^2 is always negative, if the object is to the left of F1 (x1 < 0), the image is to the right of F2 (x2 > 0)."}
              ]
            },
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In Newton's lens formula \\( x_1 x_2 = -f^2 \\), \\( x_1 \\) and \\( x_2 \\) must always have ____.", "options": ["opposite signs", "the same sign", "zero value", "imaginary values"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Because -f^2 < 0, one distance is positive and the other is negative."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In terms of extrafocal distance \\( x_1 \\) and first focal length \\( f_1 \\), lateral magnification \\( m \\) is ____.", "options": ["-f1 over x1", "x1 over f1", "f1 times x1", "f1 plus x1"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "From -y'/y = f1/x1, we get m = y'/y = -f1/x1."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "If an object is placed at a distance \\( 2f \\) to the left of \\( F_1 \\) (\\( x_1 = -2f \\)), then \\( x_2 \\) is ____.", "options": ["+f/2", "-2f", "+2f", "-f/2"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "x2 = -f^2 / x1 = -f^2 / (-2f) = +f/2."}
          ]
        },
