          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 5 marks", "title": "Question 15", "marks": 5, "q": "Calculate the radius of the first dark ring of the Fraunhofer diffraction pattern produced by a circular aperture of radius 0.02 cm at the focal plane of a convex lens of focal length 20 cm. Assume that the wavelength of light used is 600 nm."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Formula R1 = 1.22 f lambda / D (with D = 2a = 0.04 cm) and numerical result", "options": [{"t": "Formula R1 = 1.22 f lambda / (2a), D = 0.04 cm, and R1 = 3.66 x 10^-4 m (0.366 mm) (and R2 = 0.670 mm for Nov 2022)", "m": 5}, {"t": "Used radius 0.02 cm as diameter D, giving 0.732 mm", "m": 3}, {"t": "Formula only", "m": 1.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Note that aperture radius is a = 0.02 cm, so diameter D = 2a = 0.04 cm."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q15 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "First dark ring radius R1 = 0.366 mm", "body": "Know first: Single-slit Fraunhofer diffraction pattern (12). For a circular aperture of radius \\( a = 0.02\\text{ cm} \\) (diameter \\( D = 2a = 0.04\\text{ cm} = 4\\times 10^{-4}\\text{ m} \\)), the radius of the first dark ring at the focal plane (\\( f = 0.20\\text{ m} \\)) for \\( \\lambda = 600\\text{ nm} \\) is \\( R_1 = \\frac{1.22 f\\lambda}{D} = 3.66\\times 10^{-4}\\text{ m} = 0.366\\text{ mm} \\) (and the second dark ring in Nov 2022 Q15 is \\( R_2 = \\frac{2.233 f\\lambda}{D} = 0.670\\text{ mm} \\)).", "tex": "R_1 = 3.66 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ m} = 0.366\\text{ mm}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Airy dark ring radius calculation", "tex": "a = 0.02\\text{ cm} \\implies D = 2a = 0.04\\text{ cm} = 4 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ m} \\\\ f = 20\\text{ cm} = 0.20\\text{ m}, \\quad \\lambda = 600\\text{ nm} = 6 \\times 10^{-7}\\text{ m} \\\\ R_1 = f\\theta_1 = \\frac{1.22 f\\lambda}{D} = \\frac{1.22 \\times 0.20 \\times 6 \\times 10^{-7}}{4 \\times 10^{-4}} = 3.66 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ m} = 0.366\\text{ mm} \\\\ R_2 = \\frac{2.233 f\\lambda}{D} = \\frac{2.233 \\times 0.20 \\times 6 \\times 10^{-7}}{4 \\times 10^{-4}} = 6.70 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ m} = 0.670\\text{ mm}", "body": "Double the given aperture radius to get diameter D = 2a = 0.04 cm, then apply R1 = 1.22 f lambda / D. Formula 1.5 · D = 2a substitution 1.5 · final answer 2."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In terms of the aperture radius \\( a \\), the angular radius of the first dark Airy ring is ____.", "options": ["0.61 lambda over a", "1.22 lambda over a", "2.44 lambda over a", "lambda over a"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "1.22 lambda / D = 1.22 lambda / (2a) = 0.61 lambda / a."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "The linear radius \\( R_1 \\) of the first dark ring in the focal plane of a lens of focal length \\( f \\) is ____.", "options": ["f times theta1", "f divided by theta1", "theta1 divided by f", "f squared theta1"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "For small diffraction angles, R1 = f tan(theta1) ~ f * theta1."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "If the focal length \\( f \\) of the focusing lens is doubled, the radius of the Airy disc on the screen ____.", "options": ["doubles", "halves", "remains unchanged", "becomes zero"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "R1 = 1.22 f lambda / D is directly proportional to f."}
          ]
        },
