          "id": "opt-pyq-n21p-q12",
          "title": "Q12 · Advantages of matrix method in paraxial optics (2 marks)",
          "icon": "wave",
          "year": 2021,
          "month": "Nov",
          "unit": 3,
          "units": [2, 3],
          "marks": 2,
          "source": "PYQ Nov 2021 (D 10245) Q12",
          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 2 marks", "title": "Question 12", "marks": 2, "q": "What are the advantages of using matrix method in paraxial optics?"},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Systematic multiplication for multi-element systems and computer suitability", "options": [{"t": "Reduces complex multi-lens ray tracing to 2x2 matrix multiplication, separates geometry from ray coordinates, and is ideal for computers", "m": 2}, {"t": "Only one advantage stated", "m": 1}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "State how 2x2 matrices simplify multi-surface optical systems and cardinal point calculations."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q12 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Advantages of matrix methods in paraxial optics", "body": "Know first: The thin lens, principal foci and focal length. Using \\( 2\\times 2 \\) ray-transfer matrices reduces ray tracing through a complex multi-element optical system to simple sequential multiplication of translation and refraction matrices (\\( S = M_n \\dots M_2 M_1 \\)). The system matrix depends only on the optical system parameters (not the input ray), directly gives the focal lengths and cardinal points, and is ideally suited for fast computer computation.", "tex": "S = M_N M_{N-1} \\dots M_2 M_1"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Sequential matrix multiplication", "tex": "\\begin{bmatrix} \\lambda_2 \\\\ y_2 \\end{bmatrix} = S \\begin{bmatrix} \\lambda_1 \\\\ y_1 \\end{bmatrix}, \\quad S = M_N \\dots M_2 M_1, \\quad \\det S = 1", "body": "State that any cascade of lenses and spaces is represented by one 2x2 product matrix and det(S) = 1 provides a built-in algebraic check. Advantages 2."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In paraxial matrix optics, each translation or refraction operation is represented by a ____.", "options": ["2x2 matrix", "4x4 tensor", "scalar number", "3D vector"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Two paraxial coordinates (y and n*theta) require a 2x2 transformation matrix."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "If a ray passes through elements \\( M_1, M_2, M_3 \\) in that order, the overall system matrix is ____.", "options": ["M3 M2 M1", "M1 M2 M3", "M1 + M2 + M3", "M1 - M3"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Matrices are multiplied in reverse order from right to left as the ray progresses."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "The condition \\( \\det(S) = bc - ad = 1 \\) serves as a convenient check on ____.", "options": ["algebraic accuracy of the system matrix", "chromatic aberration", "light polarization", "diffraction order"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Because each elementary matrix has determinant 1, their product must also have determinant 1."}
          ]
        },
