            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 10 marks", "title": "Question 32", "marks": 10, "q": "Explain with necessary theory, the formation of Newton's rings in reflected light. How can we use this arrangement to determine the wavelength of light."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Experimental arrangement, air-film thickness t = r^2/(2R), and ring diameter theory", "options": [{"t": "Diagram, air-film sagitta formula t = r^2/(2R), path difference 2t + lambda/2, and dark/bright ring diameter equations derived", "m": 6}, {"t": "Ring equations stated without deriving t = r^2/(2R)", "m": 3}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Derive the air-film thickness t = r^2/(2R) and the diameters of bright and dark rings."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Experimental determination of wavelength lambda = (D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2) / (4 p R)", "options": [{"t": "Experimental setup with travelling microscope and formula lambda = (D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2) / (4 p R) derived", "m": 4}, {"t": "Formula stated without explanation of m and m+p rings", "m": 2}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Show how measuring the diameters of the m-th and (m+p)-th dark rings yields lambda independent of contact imperfections."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q32 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Theory of Newton's rings and wavelength determination", "body": "Know first: Colours of thin films, Newton's rings, Michelson interferometer (11). When a plano-convex lens of large radius of curvature \\( R \\) rests on a flat glass plate, a wedge-shaped air film of thickness \\( t = \\frac{r^2}{2R} \\) is formed at radius \\( r \\). For normal monochromatic illumination in reflected light, the effective path difference is \\( 2t + \\frac{\\lambda}{2} \\), giving concentric dark rings of diameter \\( D_m^2 = 4m\\lambda R \\). Measuring the diameters \\( D_m \\) and \\( D_{m+p} \\) of the \\( m \\)-th and \\( (m+p) \\)-th dark rings with a travelling microscope gives the wavelength \\( \\lambda = \\frac{D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2}{4pR} \\).", "tex": "D_m^2 = 4m\\lambda R, \\quad \\lambda = \\frac{D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2}{4pR}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Derivation of Newton's ring diameters and wavelength", "tex": "\\text{By intersecting chord theorem: } r^2 = t(2R - t) \\approx 2Rt \\implies t = \\frac{r^2}{2R} \\\\ \\text{Reflected path difference (air, } n = 1, \\cos\\theta \\approx 1\\text{): } \\Delta = 2t + \\frac{\\lambda}{2} \\\\ \\text{Dark rings: } 2t = m\\lambda \\implies \\frac{r_m^2}{R} = m\\lambda \\implies D_m^2 = 4r_m^2 = 4m\\lambda R \\\\ \\text{Bright rings: } 2t = (2m - 1)\\frac{\\lambda}{2} \\implies D_m^2 = 2(2m - 1)\\lambda R \\\\ \\text{Wavelength from } m \\text{ and } m+p \\text{ dark rings: } D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2 = 4p\\lambda R \\implies \\lambda = \\frac{D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2}{4pR}", "body": "Derive t = r^2/(2R) from the circle geometry, obtain D_m^2 = 4 m lambda R for dark rings, and subtract two ring orders to eliminate central contact error. Theory & derivation 6 · wavelength experiment 4."},
            {
              "type": "derivation",
              "label": "Derivation",
              "title": "Newton's rings diameter and wavelength formula",
              "legend": "Plano-convex lens of radius R on flat glass plate forming air film of thickness t at ring radius r.",
              "setup": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 260 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"30\" y=\"135\" width=\"200\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#e8f0fe\" stroke=\"#555\"/><path d=\"M 45 95 Q 130 175 215 95 Z\" fill=\"#f1f8ff\" stroke=\"#1a73e8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,3\"/><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"118\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"118\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"185\" y1=\"118\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"135\" stroke=\"#188038\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"122\" y=\"35\" font-size=\"10\">C (R)</text><text x=\"153\" y=\"114\" font-size=\"10\">r</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"130\" font-size=\"10\">t</text><text x=\"126\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"10\">O</text></svg>",
              "phases": ["Air-film thickness", "Reflected path difference", "Dark ring diameter", "Wavelength formula"],
              "steps": [
                {"phase": 0, "math": "r^2 = t(2R - t) \\approx 2Rt \\implies t = \\frac{r^2}{2R}", "note": "Relate ring radius r to air-film thickness t for t << R."},
                {"phase": 1, "math": "\\Delta = 2t + \\frac{\\lambda}{2} = (2m + 1)\\frac{\\lambda}{2} \\implies 2t = m\\lambda", "note": "Write the condition for destructive interference (dark rings) in reflected light."},
                {"phase": 2, "math": "2\\left(\\frac{r_m^2}{2R}\\right) = m\\lambda \\implies r_m^2 = m\\lambda R \\implies D_m^2 = 4m\\lambda R", "note": "Substitute t = r_m^2 / (2R) and express in terms of diameter D_m = 2 r_m."},
                {"phase": 3, "math": "D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2 = 4(m+p)\\lambda R - 4m\\lambda R = 4p\\lambda R \\implies \\lambda = \\frac{D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2}{4pR}", "note": "Subtract the squared diameters of the m-th and (m+p)-th dark rings."}
              ],
              "ask": [
                {"q": "For a lens of radius of curvature \\( R \\), what is the air-film thickness \\( t \\) at radial distance \\( r \\)?", "options": ["r^2 / (2R)", "2R / r^2", "r / (2R)", "2 r R"], "answer": 0, "why": "From the sagitta approximation r^2 = 2Rt, t = r^2 / (2R)."},
                {"q": "Why are Newton's rings circular in shape?", "options": ["Loci of constant air-film thickness t around the contact point are circles", "The sodium lamp is circular", "The glass plate is spherical", "The microscope objective is square"], "answer": 0, "why": "Newton's rings are fringes of equal thickness, and constant t corresponds to constant radius r."},
                {"q": "How is the diameter \\( D_m \\) of the \\( m \\)-th dark ring related to \\( m, \\lambda, R \\)?", "options": ["D_m^2 = 4 m lambda R", "D_m = 4 m lambda R", "D_m^2 = m lambda R", "D_m^2 = m / (lambda R)"], "answer": 0, "why": "Since r_m^2 = m lambda R and D_m = 2 r_m, D_m^2 = 4 r_m^2 = 4 m lambda R."},
                {"q": "Why do we measure \\( D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2 \\) instead of just \\( D_m^2 \\) in the laboratory?", "options": ["To eliminate error due to imperfect contact or dust at the center", "To avoid using a lens", "To make the central spot bright", "To eliminate wavelength"], "answer": 0, "why": "Any constant offset thickness t0 at the center cancels out when subtracting D_m^2 from D_{m+p}^2."}
              ]
            },
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Newton's rings are an example of interference fringes of ____.", "options": ["equal thickness", "equal inclination", "division of wavefront", "Fraunhofer diffraction"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Each ring traces the circular locus of constant air-film thickness t."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In the Newton's rings setup, a glass plate inclined at \\( 45^\\circ \\) is used to direct light onto the air film ____.", "options": ["normally", "at Brewster's angle", "at grazing incidence", "from below"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Horizontal rays from the collimating lens reflect downward at 90 degrees for normal incidence."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "As the order \\( m \\) increases outward from the center, the spacing between consecutive Newton's rings ____.", "options": ["decreases (rings get closer)", "increases", "remains uniform", "becomes infinite"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Because r_m varies as sqrt(m), outer rings crowd closer together."}
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "opt-pyq-n21p-q33",
          "title": "Q33 · Construction and theory of plane transmission grating and absent spectra (10 marks)",
          "icon": "wave",
          "year": 2021,
          "month": "Nov",
          "unit": 14,
          "units": [14],
          "marks": 10,
          "source": "PYQ Nov 2021 (D 10245) Q33",
          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 10 marks", "title": "Question 33", "marks": 10, "q": "Give the construction and theory of plane transmission grating. Obtain the condition for absent spectra."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Construction and N-slit Fraunhofer diffraction theory of plane transmission grating", "options": [{"t": "Construction, diagram, and derivation of I = I0 (sin beta / beta)^2 (sin N gamma / sin gamma)^2 with principal maxima d sin theta = m lambda", "m": 6}, {"t": "Grating equation and qualitative description without full intensity derivation", "m": 3}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Explain the grating construction and derive the N-slit resultant intensity and principal maxima."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Condition for absent spectra (missing orders)", "options": [{"t": "Simultaneous satisfaction of interference maximum d sin theta = m lambda and diffraction minimum b sin theta = p lambda giving (a+b)/b = m/p derived", "m": 4}, {"t": "Final ratio (a+b)/b = m/p stated without derivation", "m": 2}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Equate the angle of the m-th interference principal maximum to the p-th single-slit diffraction minimum."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q33 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Plane transmission grating theory and absent spectra", "body": "Know first: N-slit Fraunhofer diffraction pattern and grating (14). A plane transmission grating consists of a large number \\( N \\) of equidistant parallel slits of width \\( b \\) separated by opaque spaces \\( a \\) (grating element \\( d = a + b \\)). Summing the \\( N \\) diffracted waves gives \\( I = I_0 \\frac{\\sin^2\\beta}{\\beta^2}\\frac{\\sin^2 N\\gamma}{\\sin^2\\gamma} \\), with principal maxima at \\( (a + b)\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda \\). An order \\( m \\) is absent (missing) when it coincides with a single-slit diffraction minimum \\( b\\sin\\theta = p\\lambda \\), giving \\( \\frac{a + b}{b} = \\frac{m}{p} \\).", "tex": "(a+b)\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda, \\quad \\frac{a+b}{b} = \\frac{m}{p} \\text{ (absent spectra)}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Grating intensity and missing-order derivation", "tex": "A(\\theta) = A_0\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}\\frac{\\sin N\\gamma}{\\sin\\gamma}, \\quad \\beta = \\frac{\\pi b\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda}, \\quad \\gamma = \\frac{\\pi(a + b)\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda} \\\\ I = I_0\\left(\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}\\right)^2\\left(\\frac{\\sin N\\gamma}{\\sin\\gamma}\\right)^2 \\\\ \\text{Principal maximum: } (a + b)\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda \\\\ \\text{Single-slit diffraction minimum: } b\\sin\\theta = p\\lambda, \\quad p = 1, 2, 3, \\dots \\\\ \\text{Dividing: } \\frac{a + b}{b} = \\frac{m}{p} \\quad (\\text{e.g. if } a = b,\\; m = 2p = 2, 4, 6, \\dots \\text{ are absent})", "body": "Derive the N-slit intensity distribution and principal maxima, then divide the interference maximum condition by the single-slit minimum condition to obtain the absent-spectra rule. Construction & theory 6 · absent spectra 4."},
            {
              "type": "derivation",
              "label": "Derivation",
              "title": "Theory of plane transmission grating and absent spectra",
              "legend": "N parallel slits of width b and opaque spacing a diffracting parallel rays at angle theta to focus P.",
              "setup": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 260 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"65\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"65\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"65\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"125\" x2=\"65\" y2=\"145\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"175\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"82\" x2=\"175\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"118\" x2=\"175\" y2=\"105\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><ellipse cx=\"175\" cy=\"95\" rx=\"6\" ry=\"45\" fill=\"#e8f0fe\" stroke=\"#1a73e8\"/><line x1=\"175\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"235\" y2=\"65\" stroke=\"#188038\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"175\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"235\" y2=\"65\" stroke=\"#188038\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"235\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"235\" y2=\"155\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"42\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"9\">b</text><text x=\"42\" y=\"68\" font-size=\"9\">a</text><text x=\"105\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"9\">θ</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"68\" font-size=\"10\">P</text></svg>",
              "phases": ["Single-slit amplitude", "N-slit phasor sum", "Principal maxima", "Absent spectra condition"],
              "steps": [
                {"phase": 0, "math": "a_{\\text{slit}} = A_0 \\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}, \\quad \\beta = \\frac{\\pi b\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda}", "note": "Write the diffracted amplitude from each individual slit of width b at angle theta."},
                {"phase": 1, "math": "I = I_0 \\left(\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{\\sin N\\gamma}{\\sin\\gamma}\\right)^2, \\quad \\gamma = \\frac{\\pi(a+b)\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda}", "note": "Sum N slit phasors with phase step 2 gamma to obtain the resultant intensity."},
                {"phase": 2, "math": "\\gamma = m\\pi \\implies (a + b)\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda", "note": "Set sin(gamma) = 0 to find the angular positions of the m-th order principal maxima."},
                {"phase": 3, "math": "\\left.\\begin{aligned}(a+b)\\sin\\theta &= m\\lambda \\\\ b\\sin\\theta &= p\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right\\} \\implies \\frac{a+b}{b} = \\frac{m}{p}", "note": "Require the m-th interference maximum to fall on the p-th single-slit diffraction minimum."}
              ],
              "ask": [
                {"q": "What is the phase difference \\( 2\\gamma \\) between waves from corresponding points of two adjacent slits?", "options": ["(2 pi / lambda)(a + b) sin theta", "(2 pi / lambda) b sin theta", "pi a sin theta", "N pi"], "answer": 0, "why": "The path difference between adjacent slits of spacing d = a + b is (a + b) sin(theta)."},
                {"q": "Why does an interference principal maximum disappear at certain angles?", "options": ["The single-slit diffraction factor (sin beta / beta)^2 is zero at that angle", "The number of slits N becomes zero", "Refraction blocks the light", "The wavelength becomes infinite"], "answer": 0, "why": "If each individual slit sends zero resultant amplitude in direction theta, the sum of N slits is also zero."},
                {"q": "What is the condition for a single-slit diffraction minimum of order \\( p \\)?", "options": ["b sin theta = p lambda", "(a + b) sin theta = p lambda", "a sin theta = p lambda", "b cos theta = p lambda"], "answer": 0, "why": "beta = p pi gives b sin(theta) = p lambda (p = 1, 2, 3, ...)."},
                {"q": "If the opaque width equals the transparent slit width (\\( a = b \\)), which grating orders \\( m \\) are missing?", "options": ["All even orders m = 2, 4, 6, ...", "All odd orders m = 1, 3, 5, ...", "Only m = 0", "Only m = 3, 6, 9, ..."], "answer": 0, "why": "(a+b)/b = 2/1 = m/p gives m = 2p = 2, 4, 6, ..."}
              ]
            },
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "If \\( a = 2b \\) in a plane transmission grating, the orders \\( m \\) that are absent in the diffraction pattern are ____.", "options": ["3, 6, 9, ...", "2, 4, 6, ...", "1, 3, 5, ...", "none"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "(a + b)/b = 3b/b = 3 = m/p, so m = 3p = 3, 6, 9, ... are missing."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Absent spectra occur when an interference principal maximum coincides with a ____.", "options": ["single-slit diffraction minimum", "secondary maximum", "central bright fringe", "Brewster angle"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "The single-slit diffraction envelope suppresses the interference peak to zero."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "A laboratory replica transmission grating is made by pouring a thin film of collodion solution onto a ____.", "options": ["master ruled grating", "concave mirror", "Nicol prism", "soap bubble"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Stripping and mounting the hardened cellulose acetate/collodion cast produces a transmission replica."}
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "opt-pyq-n21p-q34",
          "title": "Q34 · Rectilinear propagation of light from Fresnel's half-period zones (10 marks)",
          "icon": "wave",
          "year": 2021,
          "month": "Nov",
          "unit": 15,
          "units": [15],
          "marks": 10,
          "source": "PYQ Nov 2021 (D 10245) Q34 · also Nov 2022 (D 30197) Q34, Nov 2018 Q35",
          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 10 marks", "title": "Question 34", "marks": 10, "q": "Explain the rectilinear propagation of light on the basis of Fresnel's half period zones."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Construction of Fresnel's half-period zones, zone radii, and zone areas", "options": [{"t": "Diagram of plane wavefront divided into concentric zones of path difference lambda/2, radii rn = sqrt(n b lambda), and nearly equal areas pi b lambda derived", "m": 5}, {"t": "Zone construction described without deriving radii/areas", "m": 2.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Show how a plane wavefront is divided into half-period zones with respect to observation point P and derive their radii and areas."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Alternating series summation A = A1 / 2 and explanation of rectilinear propagation", "options": [{"t": "Alternating series A = A1 - A2 + A3 - ... = A1/2 summed and rectilinear propagation explained via small first zone", "m": 5}, {"t": "Series written without explaining why light appears to travel in straight lines", "m": 2.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Sum the alternating zone amplitudes to A1/2 and explain why blocking the tiny central zone casts a sharp shadow."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q34 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Fresnel's half-period zones and rectilinear propagation", "body": "Know first: Fresnel diffraction and zone plate (15). To find the disturbance at point \\( P \\) at distance \\( b \\) from a plane wavefront \\( ABCD \\), Fresnel divided the wavefront into concentric annular zones centered at the pole \\( O \\) such that successive outer boundaries are at distances \\( b + \\frac{\\lambda}{2}, b + \\frac{2\\lambda}{2}, \\dots, b + \\frac{n\\lambda}{2} \\) from \\( P \\). The radius of the \\( n \\)-th zone is \\( r_n \\approx \\sqrt{nb\\lambda} \\) and each zone has nearly equal area \\( \\pi b\\lambda \\), while obliquity makes their amplitudes decrease slowly with alternating signs: \\( A = A_1 - A_2 + A_3 - A_4 + \\dots = \\frac{A_1}{2} \\). Since the entire wavefront produces half the amplitude of the tiny first zone surrounding the pole \\( O \\), light effectively propagates along the straight line \\( OP \\).", "tex": "r_n = \\sqrt{nb\\lambda}, \\quad A = A_1 - A_2 + A_3 - \\dots = \\frac{A_1}{2}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Fresnel zone radii, area, and alternating series", "tex": "r_n^2 = \\left(b + \\frac{n\\lambda}{2}\\right)^2 - b^2 \\approx nb\\lambda \\implies r_n = \\sqrt{nb\\lambda} \\\\ \\text{Area}_n = \\pi(r_n^2 - r_{n-1}^2) = \\pi b\\lambda \\quad (\\text{independent of } n) \\\\ A = A_1 - A_2 + A_3 - A_4 + \\dots = \\frac{A_1}{2} + \\left(\\frac{A_1}{2} - A_2 + \\frac{A_3}{2}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{A_3}{2} - A_4 + \\frac{A_5}{2}\\right) + \\dots \\\\ \\text{Since } A_2 \\approx \\frac{A_1 + A_3}{2}, \\quad A = \\frac{A_1}{2} \\\\ \\text{Because } r_1 = \\sqrt{b\\lambda} \\text{ is very small, the effective disturbance comes only from pole } O \\text{ along line } OP", "body": "Derive the zone radii rn = sqrt(n b lambda) and equal areas pi b lambda, sum the alternating series to A1/2, and conclude that only the small neighborhood of pole O contributes. Zone construction, radii & area 5 · series sum & rectilinear propagation 5."},
            {
              "type": "derivation",
              "label": "Derivation",
              "title": "Fresnel half-period zones and resultant amplitude A = A1 / 2",
              "legend": "Plane wavefront with concentric half-period zones M1, M2, ... around pole O at distance b from point P.",
              "setup": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 260 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><circle cx=\"75\" cy=\"90\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#e8f0fe\" stroke=\"#1a73e8\" stroke-width=\"1.3\"/><circle cx=\"75\" cy=\"90\" r=\"28\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1a73e8\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><circle cx=\"75\" cy=\"90\" r=\"38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1a73e8\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><circle cx=\"75\" cy=\"90\" r=\"46\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1a73e8\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"75\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"225\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"75\" y1=\"74\" x2=\"225\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><line x1=\"75\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"225\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#188038\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><text x=\"65\" y=\"94\" font-size=\"10\">O</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"94\" font-size=\"10\">P</text><text x=\"145\" y=\"104\" font-size=\"10\">b</text><text x=\"135\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"9\">b+λ/2</text><text x=\"125\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"9\">b+2λ/2</text></svg>",
              "phases": ["Zone radii", "Equal zone areas", "Alternating series", "Rectilinear limit"],
              "steps": [
                {"phase": 0, "math": "r_n^2 = \\left(b + \\frac{n\\lambda}{2}\\right)^2 - b^2 \\approx nb\\lambda \\implies r_n = \\sqrt{nb\\lambda}", "note": "Apply Pythagoras' theorem to the n-th zone boundary at distance b + n lambda / 2 from P."},
                {"phase": 1, "math": "\\text{Area}_n = \\pi r_n^2 - \\pi r_{n-1}^2 = \\pi nb\\lambda - \\pi(n-1)b\\lambda = \\pi b\\lambda", "note": "Subtract consecutive circle areas to show that every half-period zone has area pi b lambda."},
                {"phase": 2, "math": "A = A_1 - A_2 + A_3 - A_4 + \\dots = \\frac{A_1}{2} + \\left(\\frac{A_1 + A_3}{2} - A_2\\right) + \\dots = \\frac{A_1}{2}", "note": "Group the alternating zone amplitudes and use A_n = (A_{n-1} + A_{n+1})/2 to obtain A = A1 / 2."},
                {"phase": 3, "math": "\\lambda \\to 0 \\implies r_1 = \\sqrt{b\\lambda} \\to 0", "note": "Because optical wavelength lambda is very small, the effective wavefront shrinks to the pole O along line OP."}
              ],
              "ask": [
                {"q": "What is the path difference at \\( P \\) between secondary wavelets from corresponding points of two consecutive half-period zones?", "options": ["lambda / 2 (phase difference pi)", "lambda (phase difference 2 pi)", "lambda / 4", "zero"], "answer": 0, "why": "By construction, each zone lies lambda/2 farther from P than the preceding zone."},
                {"q": "Why does the amplitude \\( A_n \\) decrease slightly as the zone number \\( n \\) increases?", "options": ["Increased distance from P and the obliquity factor (1 + cos theta)", "The area of outer zones vanishes", "Outer zones absorb light", "The frequency decreases"], "answer": 0, "why": "Higher obliquity angle theta and slightly larger distance reduce outer zone amplitudes."},
                {"q": "How does the resultant amplitude \\( A \\) due to the entire unobstructed wavefront compare to that of the first half-period zone \\( A_1 \\)?", "options": ["A = A1 / 2 (intensity I = I1 / 4)", "A = A1", "A = 2 A1", "A = 0"], "answer": 0, "why": "Outer zones cancel half the contribution of the first zone, leaving A = A1 / 2."},
                {"q": "Why does light appear to travel in straight lines despite being a wave?", "options": ["Because wavelength lambda is very small, so the active central zone r1 = sqrt(b lambda) is tiny", "Because waves cannot bend", "Because outer zones add constructively", "Because air is birefringent"], "answer": 0, "why": "A small obstacle placed on the line OP blocks the first few zones and casts a sharp shadow."}
              ]
            },
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "The resultant amplitude at an axial point \\( P \\) due to an entire unobstructed plane wavefront is equal to ____.", "options": ["half the amplitude due to the first half-period zone", "twice the amplitude of the first zone", "the sum of all zone amplitudes", "zero"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "A = A1 - A2 + A3 - ... = A1 / 2."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "If a small circular disc blocks only the first half-period zone, the resultant amplitude at \\( P \\) becomes ____.", "options": ["-A2 / 2 (nearly as bright as unobstructed)", "strictly zero", "ten times brighter", "infinite"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "The remaining series -A2 + A3 - A4 + ... sums to -A2 / 2, producing Poisson's bright spot."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Consecutive Fresnel half-period zones send disturbances to point \\( P \\) that are ____.", "options": ["out of phase by pi", "in phase", "orthogonally polarized", "of different frequencies"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "A mean path difference of lambda/2 corresponds to a phase difference of pi."}
          ]
        },
        {
