            {"type": "score", "title": "Q14 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Numerical aperture (NA) of an optical fiber", "body": "Know first: Laws of reflection and refraction from Fermat's principle. The numerical aperture (NA) of an optical fiber is defined as the sine of the maximum acceptance angle \\( \\theta_a \\) at which light incident on the fiber core from air is guided by total internal reflection: \\( \\text{NA} = n_0\\sin\\theta_a = \\sqrt{n_1^2 - n_2^2} \\approx n_1\\sqrt{2\\Delta} \\). It measures the light-gathering power of the fiber.", "tex": "\\text{NA} = \\sin\\theta_a = \\sqrt{n_1^2 - n_2^2} \\approx n_1\\sqrt{2\\Delta}"},
