            {"type": "score", "title": "Q15 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Diffraction grating", "body": "Know first: N-slit Fraunhofer diffraction pattern and grating (14). A diffraction grating is an optical component consisting of a very large number \\( N \\) of equidistant, parallel, narrow slits of equal width \\( b \\) separated by opaque rulings of width \\( a \\) (typically \\( 15,000\\text{ lines/inch} \\)). The distance \\( d = a + b \\) is the grating element, and combined single-slit diffraction and multi-slit interference produce sharp principal maxima at \\( (a + b)\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda \\).", "tex": "d = a + b = \\frac{1}{N_0}, \\quad (a+b)\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Grating element and principal maxima", "tex": "d = a + b = \\frac{1}{N_0}, \\quad d\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda, \\quad m = 0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\dots", "body": "Describe the periodic ruling structure and write the grating condition. Description 1 · equation 1."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "A diffraction grating produces sharp spectral lines by combining single-slit diffraction with ____.", "options": ["N-slit interference", "total internal reflection", "optical rotation", "Brewster polarization"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Constructive interference of thousands of slits sharpens the principal maxima."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "To rule a master diffraction grating on an optically flat glass or metal plate, one uses a ____.", "options": ["fine diamond point on a ruling engine", "heated copper wire", "polaroid sheet", "calcite crystal"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "A precision diamond ruling engine cuts thousands of parallel grooves per centimeter."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "For oblique incidence at angle \\( i \\), the grating equation becomes ____.", "options": ["d (sin i + sin theta) = m lambda", "d cos i = m lambda", "d / sin theta = m lambda", "i = theta"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "The total path difference for oblique incidence is d(sin i + sin theta)."}
