            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 2 marks", "title": "Question 17", "marks": 2, "q": "State Brewster's law and what is Brewster window?"},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Brewster's law tan theta_p = n2/n1 and definition of Brewster window", "options": [{"t": "Brewster's law (tan theta_p = n2/n1, reflected & refracted rays perpendicular) and Brewster window (zero-reflection loss window for p-polarized light in lasers) explained", "m": 2}, {"t": "Only Brewster's law stated without Brewster window", "m": 1}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "State Brewster's law and explain how a Brewster-angle window transmits p-polarized light with zero reflection."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q17 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Brewster's law and Brewster window", "body": "Know first: Production of linearly polarised light (18). Brewster's law states that when unpolarized light is incident on a dielectric interface at the polarizing (Brewster) angle \\( \\theta_p \\) such that \\( \\tan\\theta_p = n_2/n_1 \\), the reflected beam is completely linearly polarized perpendicular to the plane of incidence (s-polarized), and the reflected and refracted rays are mutually perpendicular (\\( \\theta_p + r = 90^\\circ \\)). A Brewster window is a plane optical window tilted at \\( \\theta_p \\) (commonly at the ends of a gas laser tube) that transmits p-polarized light with \\( 100\\% \\) efficiency (zero reflection loss), making the laser output linearly polarized.", "tex": "\\tan\\theta_p = \\frac{n_2}{n_1} = n, \\quad \\theta_p + r = 90^\\circ"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Derivation of theta_p + r = 90 degrees", "tex": "n_1\\sin\\theta_p = n_2\\sin r \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\tan\\theta_p = \\frac{\\sin\\theta_p}{\\cos\\theta_p} = \\frac{n_2}{n_1} \\\\ \\implies \\cos\\theta_p = \\sin r = \\cos(90^\\circ - r) \\implies \\theta_p + r = 90^\\circ", "body": "State tan(theta_p) = n2/n1 and explain that a window set at theta_p has zero reflection coefficient for p-polarized light. Brewster's law 1 · Brewster window 1."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "When light is incident at the Brewster angle \\( \\theta_p \\), the angle between the reflected ray and the refracted ray is ____.", "options": ["90 degrees", "0 degrees", "45 degrees", "180 degrees"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Since theta_p + r = 90 degrees, the reflected and transmitted rays are at right angles."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "At Brewster's angle of incidence, the reflection coefficient is zero for light whose electric field vector lies ____.", "options": ["in the plane of incidence (p-polarized)", "perpendicular to the plane of incidence (s-polarized)", "at all orientations", "along the direction of propagation"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Oscillating dipoles in the medium cannot radiate along their own axis in the reflected direction."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Brewster windows are fitted at the ends of gas laser tubes (such as He-Ne lasers) to obtain a ____.", "options": ["linearly polarized laser beam with zero reflection loss", "circularly polarized beam", "diverging white beam", "diffraction pattern"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "p-polarized light suffers zero reflection loss at each pass, while s-polarized light suffers reflection losses."}
          ]
