          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 4 marks", "title": "Question 20", "marks": 4, "q": "What are conditions for producing Haidinger's fringes? How is it different from Newton's rings?"},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Conditions for producing Haidinger's fringes", "options": [{"t": "Extended monochromatic source, plane-parallel film of uniform thickness d (not extremely thin), and telescope focused at infinity", "m": 2}, {"t": "Partial conditions stated", "m": 1}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "State the uniform-thickness film, extended source, and viewing at infinity conditions."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Differences between Haidinger's fringes and Newton's rings", "options": [{"t": "Equal inclination (theta varies, d constant) vs equal thickness (t varies, theta constant); localized at infinity vs localized in the film", "m": 2}, {"t": "Only one difference stated", "m": 1}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Contrast equal inclination vs equal thickness, localization, and film geometry."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q20 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Haidinger's fringes vs Newton's rings", "body": "Know first: Colours of thin films, Newton's rings, Michelson interferometer (11). Conditions for Haidinger's fringes: (1) a plane-parallel film or plate of uniform thickness \\( d \\), (2) an extended monochromatic source so rays strike at various inclination angles \\( r \\), and (3) a telescope (or relaxed eye) focused at infinity to bring parallel reflected rays to a focus. Differences from Newton's rings: Haidinger's fringes are fringes of equal inclination (\\( d \\) constant, \\( r \\) varies) localized at infinity (as in a Michelson interferometer), whereas Newton's rings are Fizeau fringes of equal thickness (\\( t \\) varies radially, \\( r \\approx 0 \\) constant) localized within the wedge-shaped air film and viewed with a microscope.", "tex": "\\text{Haidinger: } 2nd\\cos r = m\\lambda \\text{ (equal inclination)}; \\quad \\text{Newton: } 2t(r) = m\\lambda \\text{ (equal thickness)}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Comparison of path difference equations", "tex": "\\text{Haidinger fringes (parallel film, } d = \\text{const): } \\Delta = 2nd\\cos r + \\frac{\\lambda}{2} \\; (\\text{localized at } \\infty) \\\\ \\text{Newton's rings (spherical air wedge, } r \\approx 0\\text{): } \\Delta = 2t(r) + \\frac{\\lambda}{2} = \\frac{r^2}{R} + \\frac{\\lambda}{2} \\; (\\text{localized in film})", "body": "State the three conditions for Haidinger fringes and contrast them with Newton's rings in terms of film geometry, parameter varied, and localization. Conditions 2 · differences 2."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Haidinger's fringes are interference fringes of ____.", "options": ["equal inclination", "equal thickness", "equal chromatic order", "division of wavefront"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "In a uniform plane-parallel plate, each fringe corresponds to a constant inclination angle r."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Because rays forming a given Haidinger fringe leave the plane-parallel plate parallel to one another, Haidinger fringes are localized ____.", "options": ["at infinity", "on the surface of the film", "at the light source", "inside the microscope objective"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Parallel rays intersect at infinity (or in the focal plane of a telescope objective)."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Newton's rings are localized in the air film and are observed using a ____.", "options": ["travelling microscope", "telescope focused at infinity", "polarimeter", "ruling engine"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Fizeau fringes of equal thickness are localized near the thin wedge film."}
          ]
        },
