          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 4 marks", "title": "Question 21", "marks": 4, "q": "Discuss the intensity distribution caused by Fraunhofer diffraction at a circular aperture."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Airy disc pattern, concentric rings, and angular/linear radius of first minimum", "options": [{"t": "Central bright Airy disc surrounded by concentric dark and bright rings, intensity curve/formula, and sin theta1 = 1.22 lambda / D (r1 = 1.22 f lambda / D) explained", "m": 4}, {"t": "Qualitative description and 1.22 lambda / D stated without intensity curve", "m": 2.5}, {"t": "Only formula stated", "m": 1}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Describe the Airy pattern, sketch the intensity distribution, and state the first dark ring condition."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q21 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Fraunhofer diffraction at a circular aperture (Airy pattern)", "body": "Know first: Single-slit Fraunhofer diffraction pattern (12). When plane waves of wavelength \\( \\lambda \\) pass through a circular aperture of diameter \\( D = 2a \\) and are focused by a lens of focal length \\( f \\), the diffraction pattern consists of a bright central circular patch (Airy's disc, containing \\( 84\\% \\) of total light energy) surrounded by concentric alternating dark and weak bright rings. The intensity distribution is \\( I(\\theta) = I_0 \\left[\\frac{2 J_1(v)}{v}\\right]^2 \\) with \\( v = \\frac{\\pi D\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda} \\), and the first dark ring occurs at \\( \\sin\\theta_1 = \\frac{1.22\\lambda}{D} \\) (linear radius \\( R_1 = \\frac{1.22 f\\lambda}{D} \\)).", "tex": "I = I_0\\left[\\frac{2J_1(v)}{v}\\right]^2, \\quad \\sin\\theta_1 = \\frac{1.22\\lambda}{D}, \\quad R_1 = \\frac{1.22 f\\lambda}{D}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Airy intensity distribution and ring positions", "tex": "v = \\frac{\\pi D\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda}, \\quad I(\\theta) = I_0\\left[\\frac{2J_1(v)}{v}\\right]^2 \\\\ \\text{Dark rings } (J_1(v) = 0): \\quad \\sin\\theta_1 = \\frac{1.22\\lambda}{D}, \\quad \\sin\\theta_2 = \\frac{2.233\\lambda}{D}, \\quad \\sin\\theta_3 = \\frac{3.238\\lambda}{D} \\\\ \\text{Radius of Airy disc in focal plane: } R_1 = f\\theta_1 = \\frac{1.22 f\\lambda}{D}", "body": "Explain the circular symmetry of the Airy pattern, write the intensity and dark-ring equations, and sketch the intensity distribution. Pattern & Airy disc 2 · intensity & radii equations 2."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Approximately what percentage of the total diffracted light energy is concentrated within the central Airy disc?", "options": ["84 percent", "10 percent", "50 percent", "100 percent"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "About 84% of the energy falls inside the first dark ring of the Airy pattern."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "The mathematical function that describes the amplitude of Fraunhofer diffraction by a circular aperture is the first-order ____.", "options": ["Bessel function J1(v)", "Legendre polynomial", "Hermite polynomial", "hyperbolic tangent"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Integrating over a circular aperture yields 2 J1(v) / v."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Because the objective lens of a telescope acts as a circular aperture, the image of a distant star is a ____.", "options": ["diffraction Airy disc with faint rings", "geometrical mathematical point", "square patch", "straight line"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Aperture diffraction spreads the point image into an Airy disc of radius 1.22 f lambda / D."}
          ]
