          "unit": 22,
          "units": [18, 21, 22],
          "marks": 4,
          "source": "PYQ Nov 2020 Q23",
          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 4 marks", "title": "Question 23", "marks": 4, "q": "Explain the method of producing plane, circularly and elliptically polarized light."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Production of plane (linearly) polarized light", "options": [{"t": "Passing unpolarized light through a Polaroid or Nicol prism (or reflection at Brewster's angle)", "m": 1.5}, {"t": "Partial", "m": 0.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Explain how unpolarized light is converted into linearly polarized light."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Production of circularly and elliptically polarized light using a quarter-wave plate", "options": [{"t": "Passing linearly polarized light through a QWP at phi = 45 deg for circular and phi != 0, 45, 90 deg for elliptical polarization", "m": 2.5}, {"t": "Only circular or only elliptical explained", "m": 1.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "State the orientation of the QWP optic axis relative to the incident linear polarization for both cases."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q23 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Production of linear, circular, and elliptical polarization", "body": "Know first: Production and analysis of polarised light (22). (1) Plane (linearly) polarized light is produced by passing unpolarized light through a Polaroid sheet or Nicol prism (or reflecting at Brewster's angle \\( \\theta_p = \\tan^{-1}n \\)). (2) Circularly polarized light is produced by passing linearly polarized light normally through a quarter-wave plate (QWP) with its optic axis inclined at \\( \\phi = 45^\\circ \\) to the incident electric vector, giving equal orthogonal amplitudes with a \\( \\pi/2 \\) phase shift. (3) Elliptically polarized light is produced by setting the QWP optic axis at any angle \\( \\phi \\neq 0^\\circ, 45^\\circ, 90^\\circ \\), giving unequal orthogonal amplitudes with a \\( \\pi/2 \\) phase shift.", "tex": "\\text{LP: Polaroid/Nicol}; \\quad \\text{CP: QWP at } 45^\\circ; \\quad \\text{EP: QWP at } \\phi \\neq 0^\\circ, 45^\\circ, 90^\\circ"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Equations for the three polarization states", "tex": "\\text{1. Plane polarized (after Polaroid): } E_x = E_0\\cos(kz - \\omega t), \\quad E_y = 0 \\\\ \\text{2. Circularly polarized (QWP at } \\phi = 45^\\circ\\text{): } E_x = \\frac{E_0}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sin\\omega t,\\; E_y = \\frac{E_0}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cos\\omega t \\implies E_x^2 + E_y^2 = \\frac{E_0^2}{2} \\\\ \\text{3. Elliptically polarized (QWP at } \\phi \\neq 45^\\circ\\text{): } \\frac{E_x^2}{(E_0\\sin\\phi)^2} + \\frac{E_y^2}{(E_0\\cos\\phi)^2} = 1", "body": "Describe how a Polaroid/Nicol prism produces plane polarized light, and how a QWP at 45 degrees or general angle phi produces circular or elliptical polarization. Plane 1.5 · circular & elliptical 2.5."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "When linearly polarized light passes through a quarter-wave plate with its electric vector parallel to the optic axis (\\( \\phi = 0^\\circ \\)), the emergent beam is ____.", "options": ["linearly polarized", "circularly polarized", "elliptically polarized", "unpolarized"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "At phi = 0 degrees only the extraordinary wave is excited, so the beam remains linearly polarized."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In circularly polarized light, the two orthogonal electric field components have equal amplitudes and a phase difference of ____.", "options": ["pi over 2 (or 3 pi over 2)", "zero", "pi", "2 pi"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Equal amplitudes with a 90-degree phase shift trace a circle."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "A Nicol prism eliminates the ordinary ray by ____.", "options": ["total internal reflection at a Canada balsam layer", "selective absorption", "diffraction", "scattering"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Since n_o (1.658) > n_balsam (1.55) > n_e (1.486), the o-ray is totally internally reflected."}
          ]
