            {"type": "eval", "q": "Optical path expression L = OS + SI (or OS - SI for virtual image) and paraxial expansion", "options": [{"t": "Path length expanded in small angle theta (or height h) using cosine law / binomial theorem", "m": 2}, {"t": "Partial expansion", "m": 1}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Expand the ray path OS and SI in powers of the small paraxial angle theta (or height h)."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Stationary condition dL/d theta = 0 and sign convention yielding 1/u + 1/v = 2/R", "options": [{"t": "Derivative dL/d theta = 0 equated and mirror equation 1/u + 1/v = 2/R obtained", "m": 2}, {"t": "Final formula stated without differentiation", "m": 1}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Differentiate the path length with respect to theta and apply the sign convention to get 1/u + 1/v = 2/R."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q25 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Mirror equation 1/u + 1/v = 2/R from Fermat's principle", "body": "Know first: Laws of reflection and refraction from Fermat's principle. For an axial object \\( O \\) at distance \\( x < r/2 \\) in front of a concave mirror of radius \\( r \\), the reflected ray appears to diverge from a virtual image \\( I \\) at distance \\( y \\) behind the pole \\( P \\). By Fermat's principle (Example 3.4), \\( L = OS - SI \\) is stationary for paraxial rays. Expanding in small angle \\( \\theta \\) gives \\( OS \\approx x + \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\left(\\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{r}\\right)\\theta^2 \\) and \\( SI \\approx y + \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\left(\\frac{1}{y} + \\frac{1}{r}\\right)\\theta^2 \\). Setting \\( \\frac{dL}{d\\theta} = 0 \\) yields \\( \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{y} = \\frac{2}{r} \\), which with \\( u = -x, v = +y, R = -r \\) becomes \\( \\frac{1}{u} + \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{2}{R} \\).", "tex": "\\frac{1}{u} + \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{2}{R}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Fermat derivation of concave mirror equation", "tex": "OS = \\left[(r - x)^2 + r^2 + 2r(r - x)\\cos\\theta\\right]^{1/2} \\approx x + \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\left(\\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{r}\\right)\\theta^2 \\\\ SI = \\left[(r + y)^2 + r^2 - 2r(r + y)\\cos\\theta\\right]^{1/2} \\approx y + \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\left(\\frac{1}{y} + \\frac{1}{r}\\right)\\theta^2 \\\\ L = OS - SI \\approx (x - y) + \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\left(\\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{y} - \\frac{2}{r}\\right)\\theta^2 \\\\ \\frac{dL}{d\\theta} = 0 \\implies \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{y} = \\frac{2}{r} \\\\ \\text{Sign convention } (u = -x,\\; v = +y,\\; R = -r): \\quad -\\frac{1}{u} - \\frac{1}{v} = -\\frac{2}{R} \\implies \\frac{1}{u} + \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{2}{R}", "body": "Expand OS and SI in powers of theta^2, apply dL/d theta = 0, and substitute the Cartesian signs u = -x, v = +y, R = -r. Path expansion 2 · extremum & sign convention 2."},
            {
              "type": "derivation",
              "label": "Derivation",
              "title": "Mirror equation from Fermat's principle",
              "legend": "Object O at distance x < r/2 in front of concave mirror P (center C) forming virtual image I at y behind P.",
              "setup": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 260 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"15\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"245\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><path d=\"M 155 25 Q 190 110 155 165\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1a73e8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"125\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"166\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"166\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"95\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"166\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"225\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#188038\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,3\"/><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"166\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-dasharray=\"2,2\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"10\">C</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"10\">O (x)</text><text x=\"172\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"10\">P</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"10\">I (y)</text><text x=\"168\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"10\">S</text><text x=\"95\" y=\"105\" font-size=\"9\">θ</text></svg>",
              "phases": ["Cosine-law segments", "Paraxial expansion", "Stationary condition", "Cartesian signs"],
              "steps": [
                {"phase": 0, "math": "OS = \\sqrt{x^2 + r(r - x)\\theta^2}, \\quad SI = \\sqrt{y^2 + r(r + y)\\theta^2}", "note": "Use the law of cosines in triangles OSC and ISC with cos(theta) ~ 1 - theta^2 / 2."},
                {"phase": 1, "math": "L = OS - SI \\approx (x - y) + \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\left(\\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{y} - \\frac{2}{r}\\right)\\theta^2", "note": "Apply binomial expansion (1 + epsilon)^{1/2} ~ 1 + epsilon/2 for small paraxial angle theta."},
                {"phase": 2, "math": "\\frac{dL}{d\\theta} = 0 \\implies \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{y} = \\frac{2}{r}", "note": "Set the first derivative of the optical path with respect to theta equal to zero."},
                {"phase": 3, "math": "u = -x, \\; v = +y, \\; R = -r \\implies \\frac{1}{u} + \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{2}{R}", "note": "Substitute Cartesian signs (O and C to the left of P, virtual image I to the right of P)."}
              ],
              "ask": [
                {"q": "When an object is placed closer to a concave mirror than its focus (\\( x < R/2 \\)), what kind of image is formed?", "options": ["Virtual and erect image behind the mirror", "Real and inverted image in front", "Point image at C", "No image"], "answer": 0, "why": "Reflected rays diverge and appear to come from a point I behind the mirror."},
                {"q": "What small-angle approximation is used for \\( \\cos\\theta \\) in triangles \\( OSC \\) and \\( ISC \\)?", "options": ["cos theta ~ 1 - theta^2 / 2", "cos theta ~ theta", "cos theta ~ 0", "cos theta ~ 1 + theta"], "answer": 0, "why": "Second-order Taylor expansion of cos(theta) retains the quadratic variation in path length."},
                {"q": "Which condition from Fermat's principle determines the paraxial image position?", "options": ["dL / d theta = 0 for all small theta", "L = 0", "dL / dx = 1", "theta = 90 degrees"], "answer": 0, "why": "Stationarity of the optical path with respect to ray angle theta defines the image point."},
                {"q": "Why are \\( u \\) and \\( R \\) negative while \\( v \\) is positive in this setup?", "options": ["O and C lie to the left of pole P, while virtual image I lies to the right of P", "The mirror is convex", "Refractive index is negative", "Light travels right to left"], "answer": 0, "why": "Distances to the left of P are negative and distances to the right of P are positive."}
              ]
