            {"type": "score", "title": "Q33 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Single-slit Fraunhofer diffraction intensity and extrema", "body": "Know first: Single-slit Fraunhofer diffraction pattern (12). When a plane wavefront of wavelength \\( \\lambda \\) is diffracted at angle \\( \\theta \\) by a slit of width \\( b \\), integrating the secondary wavelets across the slit gives the intensity \\( I = I_0 \\left(\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}\\right)^2 \\), where \\( \\beta = \\frac{\\pi b\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda} \\). The central principal maximum occurs at \\( \\theta = 0 \\) (\\( I = I_0 \\)). Minima of zero intensity occur when \\( \\beta = m\\pi \\) (\\( b\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda, \\; m = \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\dots \\)). Secondary maxima occur where \\( \\frac{dI}{d\\beta} = 0 \\implies \\tan\\beta = \\beta \\), approximately at \\( \\beta \\approx \\pm\\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\pm\\frac{5\\pi}{2}, \\dots \\) with relative intensities \\( \\frac{4}{9\\pi^2}, \\frac{4}{25\\pi^2}, \\dots \\).", "tex": "I = I_0\\left(\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}\\right)^2, \\quad b\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda \\text{ (minima)}, \\quad \\tan\\beta = \\beta \\text{ (secondary maxima)}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Derivation of single-slit amplitude, minima, and secondary maxima", "tex": "dE = \\frac{A_0}{b}dx\\cos(\\omega t - kx\\sin\\theta), \\quad E = \\frac{A_0}{b}\\int_{-b/2}^{b/2}\\cos(\\omega t - kx\\sin\\theta)\\,dx = A_0\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}\\cos\\omega t \\\\ \\beta = \\frac{\\pi b\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda}, \\quad I = I_0\\left(\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}\\right)^2 \\\\ \\text{1. Central maximum: } \\beta \\to 0 \\implies \\lim_{\\beta\\to 0}\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta} = 1 \\implies I = I_0 \\text{ at } \\theta = 0 \\\\ \\text{2. Minima } (I = 0): \\quad \\sin\\beta = 0 \\;(\\beta \\neq 0) \\implies \\beta = \\pm m\\pi \\implies b\\sin\\theta = \\pm m\\lambda \\; (m = 1, 2, 3, \\dots) \\\\ \\text{3. Secondary maxima: } \\frac{dI}{d\\beta} = 2I_0\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}\\frac{\\beta\\cos\\beta - \\sin\\beta}{\\beta^2} = 0 \\implies \\tan\\beta = \\beta \\\\ \\beta \\approx \\pm\\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\pm\\frac{5\\pi}{2}, \\dots \\implies I_1 = \\frac{4}{9\\pi^2}I_0 \\approx \\frac{I_0}{22.2}, \\quad I_2 = \\frac{4}{25\\pi^2}I_0 \\approx \\frac{I_0}{61.7}", "body": "Integrate the secondary wavelets over [-b/2, b/2] to obtain I = I0 (sin beta / beta)^2, solve for zeros and extrema, and sketch the intensity distribution. Intensity derivation 5 · minima, maxima & graph 5."},
            {
