            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 10 marks", "title": "Question 34", "marks": 10, "q": "Distinguish between (1) plane (2) circular and (3) elliptical polarization. Discuss the production of linearly polarized light by reflection and refraction. How would you produce and detect circularly polarized light?"},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Distinction between plane, circular, and elliptical polarization", "options": [{"t": "Locus of E-vector tip, amplitude equality, and phase difference (0/pi vs pi/2 with equal amplitudes vs pi/2 or general delta with unequal amplitudes) explained", "m": 3.5}, {"t": "Brief verbal distinction without equations", "m": 2}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Contrast the trajectory of the electric vector and the phase/amplitude conditions for the three states."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Production of linearly polarized light by reflection (Brewster's law) and refraction (pile of plates)", "options": [{"t": "Brewster reflection (tan theta_p = n) and refraction through a pile of plates at Brewster's angle explained with diagrams", "m": 3.5}, {"t": "Only reflection or only refraction explained", "m": 2}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Explain Brewster reflection (s-polarized reflected ray) and multiple refractions in a pile of plates (p-polarized transmitted ray)."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Production and detection of circularly polarized light", "options": [{"t": "Production via QWP at 45 deg and detection using rotating analyzer + QWP (to distinguish from unpolarized light) explained", "m": 3}, {"t": "Only production explained without distinguishing circular from unpolarized light", "m": 1.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Explain how a QWP produces circular polarization and how a QWP + rotating analyzer distinguishes it from unpolarized light."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q34 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "States of polarization, reflection/refraction methods, and circular polarization analysis", "body": "Know first: Production and analysis of polarised light (22). (1) In plane polarization, the electric vector \\( \\mathbf{E} \\) oscillates along a fixed straight line (\\( \\delta = 0 \\) or \\( \\pi \\)); in circular polarization, \\( \\mathbf{E} \\) has constant magnitude and rotates in a circle (\\( E_{0x} = E_{0y}, \\delta = \\pm\\pi/2 \\)); in elliptical polarization, both magnitude and direction of \\( \\mathbf{E} \\) vary as its tip traces an ellipse (\\( E_{0x} \\neq E_{0y} \\) or general \\( \\delta \\)). (2) By reflection at Brewster's angle \\( \\theta_p = \\tan^{-1}n \\), the reflected beam is completely s-polarized; by repeated refraction through a pile of glass plates inclined at \\( \\theta_p \\), the s-component is progressively reflected away and the transmitted beam becomes nearly 100% p-polarized. (3) Circularly polarized light is produced by passing linearly polarized light through a QWP at \\( 45^\\circ \\). To detect it, rotating an analyzer alone gives constant intensity (like unpolarized light); inserting a QWP before the rotating analyzer converts circularly polarized light into linearly polarized light, producing two complete extinctions per revolution.", "tex": "\\tan\\theta_p = n, \\quad \\text{CP} \\xrightarrow{\\text{QWP}} \\text{LP} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Analyzer}} 2\\text{ extinctions}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Equations for polarization states and Brewster angle", "tex": "\\frac{E_x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{E_y^2}{b^2} - \\frac{2E_x E_y}{ab}\\cos\\delta = \\sin^2\\delta \\implies \\begin{cases} \\delta = 0, \\pi \\to \\text{Plane (line)} \\\\ a = b,\\; \\delta = \\pm\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\to \\text{Circular} \\\\ a \\neq b,\\; \\delta = \\pm\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\to \\text{Elliptical} \\end{cases} \\\\ \\text{Reflection (Brewster): } \\tan\\theta_p = \\frac{n_2}{n_1}, \\quad \\theta_p + r = 90^\\circ \\\\ \\text{Detection of CP: } \\text{Rotating analyzer } (I = \\text{const}) + \\text{QWP } \\to \\text{LP } (I = I_0\\cos^2\\theta \\text{ with zero minima})", "body": "Compare the three states of polarization, explain Brewster reflection and pile-of-plates refraction, and detail the QWP + analyzer procedure for producing and detecting circular polarization. Three states 3.5 · reflection & refraction 3.5 · CP production & detection 3."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In a pile-of-plates polarizer set at Brewster's angle, the transmitted light is linearly polarized with its electric vector ____.", "options": ["in the plane of incidence (p-polarized)", "perpendicular to the plane of incidence (s-polarized)", "circularly rotating", "completely absorbed"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "At Brewster's angle, p-polarized light is 100% transmitted at every surface while s-polarized light is reflected off."},
