            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 4 marks", "title": "Question 18", "marks": 4, "q": "Derive the condition for image plane with respect to a refracting surface."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Paraxial refraction derivation or matrix derivation of n2/v - n1/u = (n2 - n1)/R", "options": [{"t": "Complete derivation of Gaussian image plane condition n2/v - n1/u = (n2 - n1)/R (via paraxial geometry or object-to-image matrix)", "m": 4}, {"t": "Partial derivation", "m": 2}, {"t": "Formula only", "m": 1}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Derive the Gaussian equation n2/v - n1/u = (n2 - n1)/R relating the object distance u and image plane distance v."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q18 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Image plane condition for a single spherical refracting surface", "body": "Know first: Refraction and reflection at a single spherical surface. For a spherical refracting surface of radius \\( R \\) separating media \\( n_1 \\) and \\( n_2 \\), paraxial Snell's law \\( n_1\\phi_1 = n_2\\phi_2 \\) with \\( \\phi_1 = \\beta + \\alpha_1 \\approx \\frac{h}{r} + \\frac{h}{x} \\) and \\( \\phi_2 = \\beta - \\alpha_2 \\approx \\frac{h}{r} - \\frac{h}{y} \\) (or setting \\( M_{21} = 0 \\) in the object-to-image matrix) gives the image plane condition \\( \\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R} \\).", "tex": "\\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Derivation of Gaussian image condition", "tex": "n_1\\sin\\phi_1 = n_2\\sin\\phi_2 \\implies n_1\\phi_1 \\approx n_2\\phi_2 \\\\ \\phi_1 = \\beta + \\alpha_1 \\approx \\frac{h}{r} + \\frac{h}{x}, \\quad \\phi_2 = \\beta - \\alpha_2 \\approx \\frac{h}{r} - \\frac{h}{y} \\\\ n_1\\left(\\frac{h}{r} + \\frac{h}{x}\\right) = n_2\\left(\\frac{h}{r} - \\frac{h}{y}\\right) \\implies \\frac{n_2}{y} + \\frac{n_1}{x} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{r} \\\\ \\text{Sign convention } (u = -x,\\; v = +y,\\; R = +r): \\quad \\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R} \\\\ \\text{(Or by matrix method: } \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ v/n_2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -P \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ -u/n_1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\implies M_{21} = \\frac{v}{n_2} - \\frac{u}{n_1} + \\frac{uv}{n_1 n_2}P = 0\\text{)}", "body": "Apply paraxial Snell's law (or the object-to-image matrix condition d = 0) at a spherical surface of radius R to obtain the image plane relation. Setup & angles 2 · derivation of image plane equation 2."},
            {
              "type": "derivation",
              "label": "Derivation",
              "title": "Image plane condition for a spherical refracting surface",
              "legend": "Paraxial ray OS in medium n1 refracting at S on spherical surface SPM (center C, radius R) to image I in medium n2.",
              "setup": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 260 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line x1=\"15\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"245\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><path d=\"M 125 30 Q 100 110 125 160\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1a73e8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"114\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"114\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"225\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"38\" x2=\"175\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,3\"/><line x1=\"114\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"114\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-dasharray=\"2,2\"/><text x=\"25\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"10\">O (-u)</text><text x=\"112\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"10\">P</text><text x=\"170\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"10\">C (R)</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"10\">I (v)</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"10\">S</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"10\">n1</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"10\">n2</text></svg>",
              "phases": ["Exterior angles", "Paraxial tangents", "Snell's law", "Sign convention"],
              "steps": [
                {"phase": 0, "math": "\\phi_1 = \\beta + \\alpha_1, \\quad \\phi_2 = \\beta - \\alpha_2", "note": "Relate incidence and refraction angles phi1, phi2 to axial angles alpha1, beta, alpha2."},
                {"phase": 1, "math": "\\phi_1 \\approx \\frac{h}{r} + \\frac{h}{x}, \\quad \\phi_2 \\approx \\frac{h}{r} - \\frac{h}{y}", "note": "Replace small angles by their tangents h/x, h/r, and h/y."},
                {"phase": 2, "math": "n_1\\left(\\frac{1}{r} + \\frac{1}{x}\\right) = n_2\\left(\\frac{1}{r} - \\frac{1}{y}\\right) \\implies \\frac{n_2}{y} + \\frac{n_1}{x} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{r}", "note": "Substitute into paraxial Snell's law n1 phi1 = n2 phi2 and cancel h."},
                {"phase": 3, "math": "u = -x, \\; v = +y, \\; R = +r \\implies \\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}", "note": "Apply the Cartesian sign convention to obtain the condition for the image plane."}
              ],
              "ask": [
                {"q": "In triangle \\( OSC \\), how is the exterior angle of incidence \\( \\phi_1 \\) expressed?", "options": ["beta + alpha1", "beta - alpha1", "alpha1 - beta", "90 - beta"], "answer": 0, "why": "Exterior angle phi1 equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles alpha1 and beta."},
                {"q": "What is the paraxial form of Snell's law \\( n_1\\sin\\phi_1 = n_2\\sin\\phi_2 \\)?", "options": ["n1 phi1 = n2 phi2", "n1 / phi1 = n2 / phi2", "phi1 = phi2", "n1 + phi1 = n2 + phi2"], "answer": 0, "why": "For small angles in radians, sin(phi) ~ phi."},
                {"q": "Why does the ray height \\( h \\) cancel out of the equation?", "options": ["Every term is proportional to h in the paraxial approximation", "h is equal to zero", "h equals R", "n1 equals n2"], "answer": 0, "why": "Cancellation of h proves that all paraxial rays from O focus at the same image distance v."},
                {"q": "Where is the second principal focus of the single refracting surface located (\\( u \\to -\\infty \\))?", "options": ["v = n2 R / (n2 - n1)", "v = R / 2", "v = (n2 - n1) / R", "v = 0"], "answer": 0, "why": "Setting 1/u = 0 in n2/v - n1/u = (n2 - n1)/R gives f2 = v = n2 R / (n2 - n1)."}
              ]
            },
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In the Gaussian formula \\( \\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R} \\), the quantity \\( \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R} \\) is called the ____.", "options": ["refracting power of the surface", "lateral magnification", "extrafocal distance", "dispersive power"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "P = (n2 - n1)/R measures the converging or diverging power of the single surface."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "For a plane refracting surface (such as viewing a coin at the bottom of a pool, \\( R \\to \\infty \\)), the image distance satisfies ____.", "options": ["v = (n2 / n1) u", "v = -u", "v = infinity", "v = 0"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "When R -> infinity, n2/v - n1/u = 0, so apparent depth / real depth = n2 / n1."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "For a single spherical refracting surface, the lateral magnification \\( m = y'/y \\) is equal to ____.", "options": ["(n1 v) / (n2 u)", "v / u", "(n2 v) / (n1 u)", "R / u"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "As shown in Eq. (4.23) of Ghatak, m = (n1 v) / (n2 u)."}
          ]
