            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 4 marks", "title": "Question 19", "marks": 4, "q": "Discuss on the resolving power of a microscope. Why oil immersion objectives are preferred in microscopes ?"},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Limit of resolution d_min = 1.22 lambda / (2 n sin theta) and resolving power 1/d_min", "options": [{"t": "Abbe's limit d_min = 1.22 lambda / (2 n sin theta) and resolving power RP = 2 n sin theta / (1.22 lambda) explained with NA = n sin theta", "m": 2.5}, {"t": "Formula stated without explaining terms", "m": 1.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "State the minimum resolvable distance d_min and resolving power 1/d_min in terms of numerical aperture n sin(theta)."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Why oil-immersion objectives are preferred", "options": [{"t": "Cedarwood oil (n ~ 1.5) increases numerical aperture n sin theta, increases resolving power by factor n, and eliminates wide-angle TIR loss", "m": 1.5}, {"t": "Mentions higher n only", "m": 0.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Explain how filling the space with oil of index n increases NA = n sin(theta) and light gathering."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q19 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Resolving power of a microscope and oil-immersion objectives", "body": "Know first: Single-slit Fraunhofer diffraction pattern (12). The limit of resolution \\( d_{\\min} \\) of a microscope is the smallest linear separation between two self-luminous (or incoherently illuminated) object points that can be just resolved: \\( d_{\\min} = \\frac{1.22\\lambda_0}{2n\\sin\\theta} = \\frac{0.61\\lambda_0}{\\text{NA}} \\), where \\( \\theta \\) is the semi-vertical angle of the cone of rays entering the objective and \\( \\text{NA} = n\\sin\\theta \\). The resolving power is \\( \\text{RP} = \\frac{1}{d_{\\min}} = \\frac{2n\\sin\\theta}{1.22\\lambda_0} \\). Oil-immersion objectives (using cedarwood oil of \\( n \\approx 1.52 \\) matching the cover glass) are preferred because they increase \\( \\text{NA} = n\\sin\\theta \\) and the resolving power by a factor of \\( n \\), while preventing total internal reflection losses at the cover slip.", "tex": "d_{\\min} = \\frac{1.22\\lambda_0}{2n\\sin\\theta}, \\quad \\text{RP} = \\frac{1}{d_{\\min}} = \\frac{2n\\sin\\theta}{1.22\\lambda_0}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Microscope resolution and oil immersion advantage", "tex": "2n d_{\\min}\\sin\\theta = 1.22\\lambda_0 \\implies d_{\\min} = \\frac{1.22\\lambda_0}{2n\\sin\\theta} = \\frac{0.61\\lambda_0}{\\text{NA}} \\\\ \\text{Resolving Power: } \\text{RP} = \\frac{1}{d_{\\min}} = \\frac{2n\\sin\\theta}{1.22\\lambda_0} = \\frac{2\\,\\text{NA}}{1.22\\lambda_0} \\\\ \\text{With oil } (n > 1): \\; \\text{NA}_{\\text{oil}} = n\\sin\\theta > \\sin\\theta \\implies \\text{RP increases by factor } n", "body": "Write Abbe's resolution condition, define resolving power 1/d_min, and explain how oil of index n increases NA and prevents reflection loss. Resolving power 2.5 · oil immersion 1.5."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "The resolving power of a microscope objective is directly proportional to its ____.", "options": ["numerical aperture n sin(theta)", "focal length", "eyepiece tube length", "wavelength lambda0"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "RP = 2 n sin(theta) / (1.22 lambda0)."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Placing cedarwood oil (\\( n \\approx 1.52 \\)) between the cover slip and the microscope objective makes the limit of resolution \\( d_{\\min} \\) ____.", "options": ["smaller (higher resolving power)", "larger (lower resolving power)", "infinite", "unchanged"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "d_min = 1.22 lambda0 / (2 n sin theta) decreases by a factor of n."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Why do ultraviolet and electron microscopes achieve much higher resolving power than visible-light microscopes?", "options": ["They use much shorter wavelengths", "They have smaller numerical apertures", "They use thicker glass lenses", "They rely on optical rotation"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Resolving power is inversely proportional to wavelength lambda."}
