          "unit": 2,
          "units": [2],
          "marks": 4,
          "source": "PYQ Nov 2019 Q23 · also Nov 2018 Q14",
          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 4 marks", "title": "Question 23", "marks": 4, "q": "Represent the effect of translation of a ray through a distance D in a homogenous medium of refractive index \\mu by the translation matrix."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Ray diagram, paraxial equations y2 = y1 + (D/mu) lambda1 and lambda2 = lambda1, and 2x2 translation matrix", "options": [{"t": "Diagram, linear equations for y2 and lambda2 = mu alpha2, and matrix T = [[1, 0], [D/mu, 1]] derived", "m": 4}, {"t": "Matrix written without derivation", "m": 2}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Draw a ray translating through distance D in medium mu and express the linear equations in 2x2 matrix form."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q23 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Translation matrix T = [[1, 0], [D/mu, 1]]", "body": "Know first: Refraction and reflection at a single spherical surface. When a paraxial ray of initial height \\( y_1 \\), slope angle \\( \\alpha_1 \\), and optical direction cosine \\( \\lambda_1 = \\mu\\alpha_1 \\) travels a straight distance \\( D \\) in a homogeneous medium of refractive index \\( \\mu \\), its slope remains unchanged (\\( \\alpha_2 = \\alpha_1 \\implies \\lambda_2 = \\lambda_1 \\)) and its height becomes \\( y_2 = y_1 + D\\tan\\alpha_1 \\approx y_1 + \\frac{D}{\\mu}\\lambda_1 \\). In matrix form, \\( \\begin{bmatrix} \\lambda_2 \\\\ y_2 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ D/\\mu & 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} \\lambda_1 \\\\ y_1 \\end{bmatrix} \\).", "tex": "\\mathcal{T} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{D}{\\mu} & 1 \\end{bmatrix}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Derivation of the translation matrix", "tex": "\\alpha_2 = \\alpha_1 \\implies \\lambda_2 = \\mu\\alpha_2 = \\mu\\alpha_1 = \\lambda_1 + 0\\cdot y_1 \\\\ y_2 = y_1 + D\\tan\\alpha_1 \\approx y_1 + D\\alpha_1 = \\frac{D}{\\mu}\\lambda_1 + 1\\cdot y_1 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix} \\lambda_2 \\\\ y_2 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ D/\\mu & 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} \\lambda_1 \\\\ y_1 \\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathcal{T} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ D/\\mu & 1 \\end{bmatrix}", "body": "Draw the ray between reference planes separated by D, write the paraxial height and slope equations, and cast them into a 2x2 matrix. Diagram & equations 2 · matrix form 2."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In free space or air (\\( \\mu = 1 \\)), the translation matrix for a distance \\( D \\) is ____.", "options": ["[[1, 0], [D, 1]]", "[[1, -D], [0, 1]]", "[[D, 0], [0, D]]", "[[0, D], [1, 0]]"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Setting mu = 1 in [[1, 0], [D/mu, 1]] gives [[1, 0], [D, 1]]."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "If a ray translates through distance \\( D_1 \\) and then \\( D_2 \\) in the same medium \\( \\mu \\), the product of their translation matrices has entry ____.", "options": ["(D1 + D2) / mu", "(D1 * D2) / mu^2", "(D1 - D2) / mu", "zero"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Lower-triangular unit-diagonal matrices add their off-diagonal reduced distances."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In the paraxial approximation, \\( \\tan\\alpha_1 \\) is replaced by ____.", "options": ["alpha1 = lambda1 / mu", "1", "zero", "mu * lambda1"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "For small angle alpha1 in radians, tan(alpha1) ~ alpha1 = lambda1 / mu."}
          ]
        },
