            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 4 marks", "title": "Question 26", "marks": 4, "q": "In a biprism experiment, interference fringes are observed at a distance of 1 m away from it. The refractive index of the material of the biprism is 1.5. If the distance between the source and the biprism is 10cm, calculate the fringe width when wave length of light used is 5000A^\\circ. Given that the biprism has refracting angle 1^0."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Virtual slit separation d, screen distance D = a+b, and fringe width beta", "options": [{"t": "d = 2a(n-1)alpha = 1.745 x 10^-3 m, D = 0.1 + 1.0 = 1.1 m, and beta = 3.15 x 10^-4 m (0.315 mm)", "m": 4}, {"t": "Used D = 1.0 m instead of 1.1 m, giving 0.286 mm", "m": 3}, {"t": "Formulas only", "m": 1}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Convert alpha = 1 degree to radians, find d = 2a(n-1)alpha and D = a + b = 1.1 m, then compute beta = lambda D / d."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q26 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Fringe width beta = 0.315 mm", "body": "Know first: Fresnel's two-mirror and Fresnel's biprism. With \\( a = 10\\text{ cm} = 0.10\\text{ m} \\), \\( b = 1.0\\text{ m} \\), \\( D = a + b = 1.10\\text{ m} \\), \\( n = 1.5 \\), and \\( \\alpha = 1^\\circ = 0.01745\\text{ rad} \\), the virtual source separation is \\( d = 2a(n - 1)\\alpha = 2(0.10)(0.5)(0.01745) = 1.745\\times 10^{-3}\\text{ m} \\). For \\( \\lambda = 5000\\text{ \\AA} = 5\\times 10^{-7}\\text{ m} \\), the fringe width is \\( \\beta = \\frac{\\lambda D}{d} = \\frac{5\\times 10^{-7}\\times 1.10}{1.745\\times 10^{-3}} = 3.15\\times 10^{-4}\\text{ m} = 0.315\\text{ mm} \\).", "tex": "\\beta = 3.15 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ m} = 0.315\\text{ mm}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Calculation of d, D, and beta", "tex": "a = 0.10\\text{ m}, \\quad b = 1.0\\text{ m}, \\quad D = a + b = 1.10\\text{ m}, \\quad n = 1.5 \\\\ \\alpha = 1^\\circ = \\frac{\\pi}{180}\\text{ rad} = 0.017453\\text{ rad} \\\\ d = 2a(n - 1)\\alpha = 2 \\times 0.10 \\times 0.5 \\times 0.017453 = 1.7453 \\times 10^{-3}\\text{ m} \\\\ \\beta = \\frac{\\lambda D}{d} = \\frac{5 \\times 10^{-7} \\times 1.10}{1.7453 \\times 10^{-3}} = 3.151 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ m} = 0.315\\text{ mm}", "body": "Convert 1 degree to radians, compute virtual slit separation d and total distance D = a + b, and evaluate beta = lambda D / d. Separation d 1.5 · D & formula 1 · result 1.5."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "If the wavelength \\( \\lambda \\) used in a biprism experiment is increased, the fringe width \\( \\beta \\) ____.", "options": ["increases proportionally", "decreases", "remains constant", "becomes zero"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "beta = lambda D / d is directly proportional to lambda."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "One angstrom (\\( 1\\text{ \\AA} \\)) is equal to ____.", "options": ["10^-10 m (or 10^-8 cm)", "10^-9 m", "10^-6 m", "10^-12 m"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "1 A = 0.1 nm = 10^-10 m = 10^-8 cm."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "If the biprism is moved closer to the slit (decreasing \\( a \\) while keeping \\( D = a + b \\) fixed), the virtual slit separation \\( d \\) decreases and the fringe width \\( \\beta \\) ____.", "options": ["increases", "decreases", "vanishes", "is unchanged"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Smaller a gives smaller d = 2a(n - 1)alpha, which increases beta = lambda D / d."}
