          "cards": [
            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 10 marks", "title": "Question 35", "marks": 10, "q": "Describe the propagation of light through an optical fibre. Examine the pulse dispersion with respect to step index fibre. Explain how pulse dispersion is reduced in Graded indexed fibre."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Propagation of light through an optical fiber (core/cladding, TIR, acceptance cone, NA)", "options": [{"t": "Core-cladding structure, TIR condition, acceptance angle, and NA = sqrt(n1^2 - n2^2) explained with diagram", "m": 3.5}, {"t": "Qualitative description only", "m": 2}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Explain total internal reflection guidance and numerical aperture."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Derivation of intermodal pulse dispersion Delta tau in a step-index fiber", "options": [{"t": "Derivation of tau_min = n1 L / c, tau_max = n1^2 L / (c n2), and Delta tau = (n1 L / c)(n1 - n2)/n2 approx (n1 L / c) Delta", "m": 3.5}, {"t": "Formula stated without derivation", "m": 2}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Derive the transit-time difference between the shortest axial path and longest critical-angle zig-zag path."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Explanation of how graded-index (GRIN) fiber reduces pulse dispersion", "options": [{"t": "Parabolic index profile n(r), sinusoidal ray paths, higher local speed v = c/n(r) away from axis equalizing transit times (Delta tau ~ n1 L Delta^2 / 2c) explained", "m": 3}, {"t": "Brief statement without explaining velocity compensation", "m": 1.5}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Explain how the lower refractive index in the outer core regions allows longer sinusoidal paths to travel faster, equalizing arrival times."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q35 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Optical fiber propagation and pulse dispersion in step vs graded-index fibers", "body": "Know first: Laws of reflection and refraction from Fermat's principle. Light launched within the acceptance cone (\\( \\sin\\theta_a = \\sqrt{n_1^2 - n_2^2} \\)) propagates along the fiber core by total internal reflection. In a step-index multimode fiber of length \\( L \\), the axial ray takes minimum time \\( \\tau_{\\min} = \\frac{n_1 L}{c} \\) while the extreme ray at the critical angle \\( \\sin\\theta_c = n_2/n_1 \\) travels path \\( L/\\sin\\theta_c \\) taking \\( \\tau_{\\max} = \\frac{n_1^2 L}{c n_2} \\), causing large intermodal pulse broadening \\( \\Delta\\tau_{\\text{step}} = \\frac{n_1 L}{c}\\frac{n_1 - n_2}{n_2} \\approx \\frac{n_1 L}{c}\\Delta \\). In a graded-index (GRIN) fiber, the core refractive index decreases parabolically with radius \\( r \\); rays oscillating farther from the axis travel longer sinusoidal paths but move mostly through lower-index regions at higher local phase velocity \\( v = c/n(r) \\), nearly equalizing transit times and reducing dispersion to \\( \\Delta\\tau_{\\text{GRIN}} \\approx \\frac{n_1 L}{2c}\\Delta^2 \\).", "tex": "\\Delta\\tau_{\\text{step}} = \\frac{n_1 L}{c}\\left(\\frac{n_1 - n_2}{n_2}\\right) \\approx \\frac{n_1 L}{c}\\Delta, \\quad \\Delta\\tau_{\\text{GRIN}} \\approx \\frac{n_1 L}{2c}\\Delta^2"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Derivation of intermodal pulse broadening and GRIN reduction", "tex": "\\text{Step-index fiber: } \\tau_{\\min} = \\frac{L}{c/n_1} = \\frac{n_1 L}{c} \\\\ \\tau_{\\max} = \\frac{L / \\sin\\theta_c}{c/n_1} = \\frac{n_1 L}{c(n_2/n_1)} = \\frac{n_1^2 L}{c n_2} \\\\ \\Delta\\tau_{\\text{step}} = \\tau_{\\max} - \\tau_{\\min} = \\frac{n_1 L}{c}\\left(\\frac{n_1 - n_2}{n_2}\\right) \\approx \\frac{n_1 L}{c}\\Delta \\\\ \\text{Graded-index fiber: } n^2(r) = n_1^2\\left[1 - 2\\Delta\\left(\\frac{r}{a}\\right)^2\\right], \\quad v(r) = \\frac{c}{n(r)} \\\\ \\text{Optical path } \\int n(r)\\,ds \\text{ is nearly stationary for all rays} \\implies \\Delta\\tau_{\\text{GRIN}} \\approx \\frac{n_1 L}{2c}\\Delta^2 \\ll \\Delta\\tau_{\\text{step}}", "body": "Explain TIR propagation, derive Delta tau = tau_max - tau_min for a step-index fiber, and show how radial velocity compensation in a parabolic GRIN core equalizes transit times. Propagation 3.5 · step-index dispersion 3.5 · GRIN reduction 3."},
            {
              "type": "derivation",
              "label": "Derivation",
              "title": "Intermodal pulse dispersion in a step-index fiber",
              "legend": "Comparison of fast axial ray (path L) and slow extreme meridional ray at critical angle theta_c (path L / sin theta_c).",
              "setup": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 260 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"25\" y=\"40\" width=\"210\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#f8f9fa\" stroke=\"#1a73e8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"25\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"235\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#188038\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><polyline points=\"25,90 75,40 175,140 235,80\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d93025\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"75\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"75\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,3\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"84\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#188038\">Axial ray (τmin)</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"9\">θc</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"32\" font-size=\"10\">Cladding n2</text><text x=\"125\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"10\">Length L (Core n1)</text></svg>",
              "phases": ["Axial ray time", "Extreme ray path", "Transit time difference", "GRIN compensation"],
              "steps": [
                {"phase": 0, "math": "\\tau_{\\min} = \\frac{L}{c/n_1} = \\frac{n_1 L}{c}", "note": "Compute the minimum transit time along the shortest axial path of length L."},
                {"phase": 1, "math": "L_{\\max} = \\frac{L}{\\sin\\theta_c} = \\frac{n_1}{n_2}L \\implies \\tau_{\\max} = \\frac{L_{\\max}}{c/n_1} = \\frac{n_1^2 L}{c n_2}", "note": "Compute the maximum transit time for the steepest guided ray incident at the critical angle sin(theta_c) = n2/n1."},
                {"phase": 2, "math": "\\Delta\\tau_{\\text{step}} = \\tau_{\\max} - \\tau_{\\min} = \\frac{n_1 L}{c}\\left(\\frac{n_1 - n_2}{n_2}\\right) \\approx \\frac{n_1 L}{c}\\Delta", "note": "Subtract tau_min from tau_max to obtain the intermodal pulse broadening."},
                {"phase": 3, "math": "n(r) \\text{ parabolic} \\implies v(r) = \\frac{c}{n(r)} \\text{ higher at larger } r \\implies \\Delta\\tau_{\\text{GRIN}} \\approx \\frac{n_1 L}{2c}\\Delta^2", "note": "In a parabolic GRIN fiber, higher speed in the outer core compensates for the longer curved path."}
              ],
              "ask": [
                {"q": "Which ray takes the shortest time \\( \\tau_{\\min} \\) to travel through a step-index fiber of length \\( L \\)?", "options": ["The axial ray travelling straight along the center", "The ray incident at the critical angle", "The ray refracted into the cladding", "The skew ray near the wall"], "answer": 0, "why": "In a uniform-index core, speed c/n1 is constant everywhere, so the shortest geometric path L takes the least time."},
                {"q": "If a meridional ray makes angle \\( 90^\\circ - \\theta_c \\) with the fiber axis, what is its total geometric path length over fiber length \\( L \\)?", "options": ["L / sin(theta_c)", "L sin(theta_c)", "L cos(theta_c)", "L"], "answer": 0, "why": "Path length = L / cos(90 - theta_c) = L / sin(theta_c)."},
                {"q": "Substituting \\( \\sin\\theta_c = n_2 / n_1 \\), what is the expression for \\( \\tau_{\\max} - \\tau_{\\min} \\)?", "options": ["(n1 L / c)(n1 - n2)/n2", "(n2 L / c)(n1 + n2)", "L / c", "zero"], "answer": 0, "why": "(n1^2 L)/(c n2) - (n1 L)/c = (n1 L / c)(n1 - n2)/n2."},
