            {"type": "score", "title": "Q13 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Double refraction (birefringence)", "body": "Know first: Double refraction and Huygens' explanation (20). Double refraction (birefringence) is the phenomenon in which a single unpolarized ray of light entering an anisotropic crystal (such as calcite or quartz) splits into two refracted rays that are linearly polarized in mutually perpendicular directions: the ordinary ray (o-ray), which obeys Snell's law, and the extraordinary ray (e-ray), whose speed varies with direction.", "tex": "\\text{Unpolarized ray} \\to \\text{o-ray } (n_o) + \\text{e-ray } (n_{re}(\\theta))"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Refractive indices of o-ray and e-ray", "tex": "\\frac{\\sin i}{\\sin r_o} = n_o \\text{ (const)}, \\quad n_{re}(\\theta) = \\sqrt{n_o^2\\cos^2\\theta + n_e^2\\sin^2\\theta}", "body": "Define double refraction and distinguish the ordinary ray and extraordinary ray. Definition 1 · o-ray & e-ray 1."},
