            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 2 marks", "title": "Question 16", "marks": 2, "q": "What are Newton's rings ? What are its uses ?"},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Definition of Newton's rings and at least two experimental uses", "options": [{"t": "Concentric circular fringes of equal thickness formed in air film between a plano-convex lens and flat glass plate defined, and uses (measuring wavelength, refractive index of liquid, radius of curvature/optical flatness) listed", "m": 2}, {"t": "Only definition or only uses stated", "m": 1}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Define Newton's rings and state their primary laboratory applications."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q16 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Newton's rings and their applications", "body": "Know first: Colours of thin films, Newton's rings, Michelson interferometer (11). Newton's rings are concentric alternating bright and dark circular interference fringes of equal thickness formed in the thin wedge-shaped film enclosed between a plano-convex lens of large radius of curvature and a plane glass plate. Uses: (1) to determine the wavelength \\( \\lambda \\) of monochromatic light, (2) to measure the refractive index \\( \\mu \\) of a transparent liquid, (3) to find the radius of curvature \\( R \\) of a lens, and (4) to test the optical flatness/sphericity of surfaces.", "tex": "\\lambda = \\frac{D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2}{4pR}, \\quad \\mu = \\frac{(D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2)_{\\text{air}}}{(D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2)_{\\text{liquid}}}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Formulas for wavelength and liquid refractive index", "tex": "D_m^2 = \\frac{4m\\lambda R}{\\mu} \\implies \\lambda = \\frac{D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2}{4pR} \\;(\\mu = 1), \\quad \\mu = \\frac{(D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2)_{\\text{air}}}{(D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2)_{\\text{liquid}}}", "body": "Define Newton's rings and state how they are used to measure wavelength, liquid refractive index, and lens curvature. Definition 1 · uses 1."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In the Newton's rings method, the refractive index \\( \\mu \\) of a liquid is given by the ratio of \\( (D_{m+p}^2 - D_m^2) \\) in air to that in ____.", "options": ["the liquid", "vacuum squared", "mercury", "a prism"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Since D_m^2 is inversely proportional to mu, mu = (Delta D^2)_air / (Delta D^2)_liquid."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "If the glass plate beneath the plano-convex lens is replaced by a front-surface silvered plane mirror, the central spot in reflected light becomes ____.", "options": ["bright", "dark", "square", "invisible"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Both reflections now occur at denser media (or with identical phase shifts), making the central spot bright."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Newton's rings are formed by interference of waves reflected from the ____.", "options": ["lower curved surface of the lens and upper surface of the glass plate", "top flat surface of the lens and the microscope objective", "collimator and sodium lamp", "two slits"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "The thin air film is bounded by the curved bottom of the lens and the flat top of the optical plate."}
          ]
