            {"type": "question", "label": "PYQ · 2 marks", "title": "Question 17", "marks": 2, "q": "How are the hologram classified ? Explain."},
            {"type": "eval", "q": "Classification into transmission vs reflection, amplitude vs phase, and thin (plane) vs thick (volume) holograms", "options": [{"t": "At least two classifications (e.g. transmission vs reflection, amplitude vs phase, thin/plane vs thick/volume, or Fresnel vs Fraunhofer/Fourier) explained", "m": 2}, {"t": "Only names listed without explanation", "m": 1}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "State how holograms are classified by geometry, modulation mechanism, and emulsion thickness."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q17 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Classification of holograms", "body": "Know first: Interference of light waves. Holograms are classified by: (1) Illumination mode: Transmission holograms (reference and object beams enter from the same side; viewed by transmitted laser light) and Reflection (Denisyuk) holograms (beams enter from opposite sides; can be viewed in white light by Bragg reflection). (2) Modulation type: Amplitude holograms (spatial variation in absorption/transmittance) and Phase holograms (bleached variation in refractive index or thickness, giving higher diffraction efficiency). (3) Thickness: Thin (plane/surface) holograms and Thick (volume) holograms.", "tex": "\\text{Transmission / Reflection; Amplitude / Phase; Thin / Volume}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Amplitude vs phase transmittance", "tex": "T_{\\text{amp}}(x,y) = |T(x,y)|, \\quad T_{\\text{phase}}(x,y) = e^{i\\phi(x,y)}", "body": "Explain the classification into transmission/reflection, amplitude/phase, and thin/volume holograms. Classification & explanation 2."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "A hologram that modulates the phase of the readout wave via refractive-index or thickness variations rather than absorption is a ____.", "options": ["phase hologram", "amplitude hologram", "pinhole camera", "wire-grid polarizer"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Bleached phase holograms avoid absorption loss and achieve much higher diffraction efficiency."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "A reflection (volume) hologram recorded with reference and object beams entering from opposite sides can be reconstructed using ____.", "options": ["an ordinary white-light point source", "X-rays only", "sound waves", "radio waves"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Internal Bragg planes selectively reflect only the recording wavelength from white light."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Gabor's original hologram, where the reference wave and object wave travel along the same axis, is called an ____.", "options": ["in-line (coaxial) hologram", "off-axis Leith-Upatnieks hologram", "rainbow hologram", "computer-generated hologram"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Gabor used a single axial beam where unscattered light served as the in-line reference wave."}
          ]
