            {"type": "eval", "q": "Definition of polarization, unpolarized vs polarized light, and transverse nature", "options": [{"t": "Confinement/asymmetry of electric field vibrations perpendicular to propagation direction defined, unpolarized vs plane/circular/elliptical polarized light explained with diagrams, and transverse wave proof noted", "m": 4}, {"t": "Definition and basic description without diagrams or transverse wave explanation", "m": 2.5}, {"t": "Brief definition only", "m": 1}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Define polarization of electromagnetic waves, contrast unpolarized and polarized light, and explain how it proves light is transverse."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q22 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Polarization of light and transverse wave nature", "body": "Know first: Polarisation: introduction and transverse wave nature (17). Polarization is the phenomenon of restricting the vibrations of the transverse electric field vector \\( \\mathbf{E} \\) of a light wave to a specific orientation or regular path in the plane perpendicular to the direction of propagation \\( \\mathbf{k} \\). In ordinary unpolarized light (from the sun or a lamp), the direction of \\( \\mathbf{E} \\) in the transverse \\( x\\text{-}y \\) plane changes randomly every \\( \\sim 10^{-8}\\text{ s} \\). When passed through a polarizer (like a Polaroid or tourmaline crystal), \\( \\mathbf{E} \\) oscillates along a single fixed line (linearly polarized light). Because longitudinal waves are symmetrical about their propagation axis, polarization conclusively proves that light waves are transverse.", "tex": "E_x = E_0\\cos(kz - \\omega t), \\; E_y = 0, \\; E_z = 0 \\quad (\\mathbf{E} \\perp \\mathbf{k})"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Transverse electromagnetic wave equations", "tex": "\\text{Unpolarized: } E_x(t), E_y(t) \\text{ have random relative phase; } E_z = 0 \\\\ \\text{x-polarized: } E_x = E_0\\cos(kz - \\omega t), \\quad E_y = 0, \\quad E_z = 0 \\\\ \\text{Malus's law after analyzer at angle } \\theta: \\quad I = I_0\\cos^2\\theta", "body": "Define polarization, contrast random unpolarized vibrations with restricted polarized vibrations using diagrams, and explain the proof of transverse character. Definition 1.5 · unpolarized vs polarized & diagrams 1.5 · transverse nature 1."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "The phenomenon of polarization proves conclusively that light waves are ____.", "options": ["transverse", "longitudinal", "stationary", "corpuscular"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Only transverse waves have directional asymmetry about the line of propagation."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In a plane electromagnetic light wave, the optical effects (and polarization direction) are identified with the ____.", "options": ["electric field vector E", "magnetic field vector H", "propagation vector k", "gravitational field"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "The electric vector E exerts the primary force on electrons in matter and detectors."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Sound waves in air cannot be polarized because they are ____.", "options": ["longitudinal waves", "electromagnetic waves", "too fast", "monochromatic"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Longitudinal displacements are along the direction of propagation, so rotating a slit does not block them."}
          ]
