            {"type": "eval", "q": "Comparison of refractive indices, wave speeds, Huygens wavefronts, and examples", "options": [{"t": "Four points compared: (1) ne > no vs ne < no, (2) vo > ve vs vo < ve, (3) ellipsoid inside sphere vs outside sphere (with diagrams), and (4) examples (quartz vs calcite)", "m": 4}, {"t": "Two or three points compared", "m": 2.5}, {"t": "Only one point compared", "m": 1}, {"t": "Missing or wrong", "m": 0}], "why": "Compare refractive indices, velocities, wavefront geometry, and examples for positive and negative uniaxial crystals."},
            {"type": "score", "title": "Q23 · Your marks"},
            {"type": "info", "label": "Answer", "title": "Positive vs negative uniaxial crystals", "body": "Know first: Double refraction and Huygens' explanation (20). (1) Refractive index: In a positive uniaxial crystal, \\( n_e > n_o \\) (birefringence \\( n_e - n_o > 0 \\)); in a negative uniaxial crystal, \\( n_e < n_o \\) (\\( n_e - n_o < 0 \\)). (2) Wave velocity: Off the optic axis, \\( v_o > v_e \\) in a positive crystal (e-ray is slow), whereas \\( v_e > v_o \\) in a negative crystal (e-ray is fast). (3) Huygens wave surfaces: The e-ray ellipsoid of revolution lies inside the o-ray sphere in a positive crystal, and outside the o-ray sphere in a negative crystal (both touching along the optic axis). (4) Examples: Quartz and ice are positive; calcite and tourmaline are negative.", "tex": "\\text{Positive: } n_e > n_o,\\; v_o > v_e \\text{ (Quartz)}; \\quad \\text{Negative: } n_e < n_o,\\; v_e > v_o \\text{ (Calcite)}"},
            {"type": "math", "label": "Solution", "title": "Summary comparison of uniaxial crystals", "tex": "\\text{1. Index: } \\text{Positive: } n_e > n_o; \\quad \\text{Negative: } n_e < n_o \\\\ \\text{2. Velocity } (\\theta = \\pi/2): \\quad \\text{Positive: } v_e = \\frac{c}{n_e} < v_o = \\frac{c}{n_o}; \\quad \\text{Negative: } v_e = \\frac{c}{n_e} > v_o = \\frac{c}{n_o} \\\\ \\text{3. Wavefronts: Positive: ellipsoid inside sphere; Negative: ellipsoid outside sphere} \\\\ \\text{4. Examples: Positive: quartz, ice, rutile; Negative: calcite, tourmaline, ruby}", "body": "Contrast refractive indices, velocities, wavefront shapes (with sketches), and crystal examples. Four comparison points 4."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In a positive uniaxial crystal, the extraordinary ray is refracted ____.", "options": ["more than the ordinary ray (closer to the normal)", "less than the ordinary ray", "at 90 degrees always", "back into air"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Because n_e > n_o in a positive crystal, the e-ray bends more towards the normal than the o-ray."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "In both positive and negative uniaxial crystals, the number of optic axes is ____.", "options": ["one", "two", "three", "zero"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "Uniaxial crystals have a single optic axis direction along which v_o = v_e."},
            {"type": "mcq", "q": "Crystals like mica and topaz that have two directions along which the two rays travel at equal speed are called ____.", "options": ["biaxial crystals", "uniaxial crystals", "isotropic crystals", "amorphous glasses"], "answer": 0, "time": 45, "practice": "remember-word", "why": "As noted in the footnote on page 22.15 of Ghatak, crystals with two optic axes are biaxial."}
